window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2017-06-2", "source": "2017年06月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part,Suppose you are asked to give advice on whether to attend a vocational college or a university, write an essay to state your opinion. You are required to write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "When young people stand at the crossroads between a vocational college and a university, my advice is clear:\nchoose the university whenever it is within reach.\nAdmittedly, vocational colleges have real strengths. They train practical, job-ready skills and often lead to\nemployment faster, which appeals to students eager to earn early. However, I still believe a university offers\ndeeper and more lasting benefits. Firstly, a university builds a broad academic foundation and cultivates critical\nthinking, abilities that keep paying off as careers evolve. A friend of mine who studied engineering at\nuniversity was later promoted to a management role precisely because he could analyse problems, not merely\noperate machines. Moreover, the wider network and long-term earning potential a degree brings usually\noutweigh the short-term gap in starting salaries.\nIn contrast, narrow technical training may limit future growth once industries change. Therefore, unless a\nstudent has a strong practical calling, I would firmly recommend the university path for its broader and more\nenduring rewards."}, "translation": {"prompt": "宋朝始于960年，一直延续到1279年。这一时期，中国经济大幅增长，成为世界上最先进的经济体，科学、技术、哲学和数学蓬勃发展。宋代中国是世界历史上首先发行纸币的国家。宋朝还最早使用火药并发明了活字(movable-type)印刷。人口增长迅速，越来越多的人住进城市，那里有热闹的娱乐场所。社会生活多种多样。人们聚集在一起观看和交易珍贵艺术品。宋朝的政府体制在当时也是先进的。政府官员均通过竞争性考试选拔任用。", "sample": "The Song Dynasty began in 960 and lasted until 1279. During this period, the Chinese economy grew significantly, becoming the most advanced in the world, while science, technology, philosophy and mathematics all flourished. Song China was the first country in the world to issue paper money. The Song Dynasty was also the first to use gunpowder and invented movable-type printing. The population grew rapidly, and more and more people moved into cities, where there were lively entertainment venues. In addition, social life was diverse: people gathered to view and trade precious works of art. The Song government system was also advanced for its time, as government officials were all selected through competitive examinations."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2017-06-2/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Half of your brain stays alert and prepared for danger when you sleep in a new place, a study has revealed. This phenomenon is often  __26__  to as the \"first-night-effect\". Researchers from Brown University found that a network in the left hemisphere of the brain \"remained more active\" than the network in the right side of the brain. Playing sounds into the right ears (stimulating the left hemisphere) of  __27__  was more likely to wake them up than if the noises were played into their left ear.\nIt was  __28__  observed that the left side of the brain was more active during deep sleep. When the researchers repeated the laboratory experiment on the second and third nights they found the left hemisphere could not be stimulated in the same way during deep sleep. The researchers explained that the study demonstrated when we are in a  __29__  environment the brain partly remains alert so that humans can defend themselves against any  __30__  danger.\nThe researchers believe this is the first time that the \"first-night-effect\" of different brain states has been  __31__  in humans. It isn't, however, the first time it has ever been seen. Some animal  __32__  also display this phenomenon. For example, dolphins, as well as other  __33__  animals, shut down one hemisphere of the brain when they go to sleep. A previous study noted that dolphins always  __34__  control their breathing. Without keeping the brain active while sleeping, they would probably drown. But, as the human study suggests, another reason for dolphins keeping their eyes open during sleep is that they can look out for  __35__  while asleep. It also keeps their physiological processes working.", "matchIntro": "Elite Math Competitions Struggle to Diversify Their Talent Pool", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Interest in elite high school math competitions has grown in recent years, and in light of last summer's U.S. win at the International Math Olympiad (IMO)—the first for an American team in more than two decades—the trend is likely to continue."}, {"label": "B", "text": "But will such contests, which are overwhelmingly dominated by Asian and white students from middle-class and affluent families, become any more diverse? Many social and cultural factors play roles in determining which promising students get on the path toward international math recognition. But efforts are in place to expose more black, Hispanic, and low-income students to advanced math, in the hope that the demographic pool of high-level contenders will eventually begin to shift and become less exclusive."}, {"label": "C", "text": "\"The challenge is if certain types of people are doing something, it's difficult for other people to break into it,\" said Po-Shen Loh, the head coach of last year's winning U.S. Math Olympiad team. Participation grows through friends and networks and if \"you realize that's how they're growing, you can start to take action\" and bring in other students, he said."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Most of the training for advanced-math competitions happens outside the confines of the normal school day. Students attend after-school clubs, summer camps, online forums and classes, and university-based \"math circles\", to prepare for the competitions."}, {"label": "E", "text": "One of the largest feeders for high school math competitions—including those that eventually lead to the IMO—is a middle school program called Math Counts. About 100,000 students around the country participate in the program's competition series, which culminates in a national game-show-style contest held each May. The most recent one took place last week in Washington, D.C. Students join a team through their schools, which provide a volunteer coach and pay a nominal fee to send students to regional and state competitions. The 224 students who make it to the national competition get an all-expenses-paid trip."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Nearly all members of last year's winning U.S. IMO team took part in Math Counts as middle school students, as did Loh, the coach. \"Middle school is an important age because students have enough math capability to solve advanced problems, but they haven't really decided what they want to do with their lives,\" said Loh. \"They often get hooked then.\""}, {"label": "G", "text": "Another influential feeder for advanced-math students is an online school called Art of Problem Solving, which began about 13 years ago and now has 15,000 users. Students use forums to chat, play games, and solve problems together at no cost, or they can pay a few hundred dollars to take courses with trained teachers. According to Richard Rusczyk, the company founder, the six U.S. team members who competed at the IMO last year collectively took more than 40 courses on the site. Parents of advanced-math students and Math Counts coaches say the children are on the website constantly."}, {"label": "H", "text": "There are also dozens of summer camps—many attached to universities—that aim to prepare elite math students. Some are pricey—a three-week intensive program can cost $4,500 or more—but most offer scholarships. The Math Olympiad Summer Training Program is a three-week math camp held by the Mathematical Association of America that leads straight to the international championship and is free for those who make it. Only about 50 students are invited based on their performance on written tests and at the USA Math Olympiad."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Students in university towns may also have access to another lever for involvement in accelerated math: math circles. In these groups, which came out of an Eastern European tradition of developing young talent, professors teach promising K-12 students advanced mathematics for several hours after school or on weekends. The Los Angeles Math Circle, held at the University of California, Los Angeles, began in 2007 with 20 students and now has more than 250. \"These math circles cost nothing, or they're very cheap for students to get involved in, but you have to know about them,\" said Rusczyk. \"Most people would love to get students from more underserved populations, but they just can't get them in the door. Part of it is communication; part of it is transportation.\""}, {"label": "J", "text": "It's no secret in the advanced-math community that diversity is a problem. According to Mark Saul, the director of competitions for the Mathematical Association of America, not a single African-American or Hispanic student—and only a handful of girls—has ever made it to the Math Olympiad team in its 50 years of existence. Many schools simply don't prioritize academic competitions. \"Do you know who we have to beat?\" asked Saul. \"The football team, the basketball team—that's our competition for resources, student time, attention, school dollars, parent efforts, school enthusiasm.\""}, {"label": "K", "text": "Teachers in low-income urban and rural areas with no history of participating in math competitions may not know about advanced-math opportunities like Math Counts—and those who do may not have support or feel trained to lead them."}, {"label": "L", "text": "But there are initiatives in place to try to get more underrepresented students involved in accelerated math. A New York City-based nonprofit called Bridge to Enter Mathematics runs a residential summer program aimed at getting underserved students, mostly black and Hispanic, working toward math and science careers. The summer after 7th grade, students spend three weeks on a college campus studying advanced math for seven hours a day. Over the next five years, the group helps the students get into other elite summer math programs, high-performing high schools, and eventually college. About 250 students so far have gone through the program, which receives funding from the Jack Kent Cooke Foundation."}, {"label": "M", "text": "\"If you look at a lot of low-income communities in the United States, there are programs that are serving them, but they're primarily centered around 'Let's get these kids' grades up', and not around 'Let's get these kids access to the same kinds of opportunities as more-affluent kids,'\" said Daniel Zaharopol, the founder and executive director of the program. \"We're trying to create that pathway.\" Students apply to the program directly through their schools. \"We want to reach parents who are not plugged into the system,\" said Zaharopol."}, {"label": "N", "text": "In the past few years, Math Counts added two new middle school programs to try to diversify its participant pool—the National Math Club and the Math Video Challenge. Schools or teachers who sign up for the National Math Club receive a kit full of activities and resources, but there's no special teacher training and no competition attached."}, {"label": "O", "text": "The Math Video Challenge is a competition, but a collaborative one. Teams of four students make a video illustrating a math problem and its real-world application. After the high-pressure Countdown round at this year's national Math Counts competition, in which the top 12 students went head to head solving complex problems in rapid fire, the finalists for the Math Video Challenge took the stage to show their videos. The demographics of that group looked quite different from those in the competition round—of the 16 video finalists, 13 were girls and eight were African-American students. The video challenge does not put individual students on the hot seat—so it's less intimidating by design. It also adds the element of artistic creativity to attract a new pool of students who may not see themselves as \"math people\"."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "We live today indebted to McCardell, Cashin, Hawes, Wilkins, and Maxwell, and other women who liberated American fashion from the confines of Parisian design. Independence came in tying, wrapping, storing, harmonizing, and rationalizing that wardrobe. These designers established the modern dress code, letting playsuits and other active wear outfits suffice for casual clothing, allowing pants to enter the wardrobe, and prizing rationalism and versatility in dress, in contradiction to dressing for an occasion or allotment of the day. Fashion in America was logical and answerable to the will of the women who wore it. Implicitly or explicitly, American fashion addressed a democracy, whereas traditional Paris-based fashion was prescriptive and imposed on women, willing or not.\nIn an earlier time, American fashion had also followed the dictates of Paris, or even copied and pirated specific French designs. Designer sportswear was not modeled on that of Europe, as \"modern art\" would later be; it was genuinely invented and developed in America. Its designers were not high-end with supplementary lines. The design objective and the business commitment were to sportswear, and the distinctive traits were problem-solving ingenuity and realistic lifestyle applications. Ease of care was most important: summer dresses and outfits, in particular, were chiefly cotton, readily capable of being washed and pressed at home. Closings were simple, practical, and accessible, as the modern woman depended on no personal maid to dress her. American designers prized resourcefulness and the freedom of women who wore the clothing.\nMany have argued that the women designers of this time were able to project their own clothing values into a new style. Of course, much of this argument in the 1930s-40s was advanced because there was little or no experience in justifying apparel(服装) on the basis of utility. If Paris was cast aside, the tradition of beauty was also to some degree slighted. Designer sportswear would have to be verified by a standard other than that of pure beauty; the emulation of a designer's life in designer sportswear was a crude version of this relationship. The consumer was ultimately to be mentioned as well, especially by the likes of Dorothy Shaver, who could point to the sales figures at Lord & Taylor.\nCould utility alone justify the new ideas of the American designers? Fashion is often regarded as a pursuit of beauty, and some cherished fashion's trivial relationship to the fine arts. What the designers of the American sportswear proved was that fashion is a genuine design art, answering to the demanding needs of service. Of course these practical, insightful designers have determined the course of late twentieth-century fashion. They were the pioneers of gender equity, in their useful, adaptable clothing, which was both made for the masses and capable of self-expression.", "47": "We live today indebted to McCardell, Cashin, Hawes, Wilkins, and Maxwell, and other women who liberated American fashion from the confines of Parisian design. Independence came in tying, wrapping, storing, harmonizing, and rationalizing that wardrobe. These designers established the modern dress code, letting playsuits and other active wear outfits suffice for casual clothing, allowing pants to enter the wardrobe, and prizing rationalism and versatility in dress, in contradiction to dressing for an occasion or allotment of the day. Fashion in America was logical and answerable to the will of the women who wore it. Implicitly or explicitly, American fashion addressed a democracy, whereas traditional Paris-based fashion was prescriptive and imposed on women, willing or not.\nIn an earlier time, American fashion had also followed the dictates of Paris, or even copied and pirated specific French designs. Designer sportswear was not modeled on that of Europe, as \"modern art\" would later be; it was genuinely invented and developed in America. Its designers were not high-end with supplementary lines. The design objective and the business commitment were to sportswear, and the distinctive traits were problem-solving ingenuity and realistic lifestyle applications. Ease of care was most important: summer dresses and outfits, in particular, were chiefly cotton, readily capable of being washed and pressed at home. Closings were simple, practical, and accessible, as the modern woman depended on no personal maid to dress her. American designers prized resourcefulness and the freedom of women who wore the clothing.\nMany have argued that the women designers of this time were able to project their own clothing values into a new style. Of course, much of this argument in the 1930s-40s was advanced because there was little or no experience in justifying apparel(服装) on the basis of utility. If Paris was cast aside, the tradition of beauty was also to some degree slighted. Designer sportswear would have to be verified by a standard other than that of pure beauty; the emulation of a designer's life in designer sportswear was a crude version of this relationship. The consumer was ultimately to be mentioned as well, especially by the likes of Dorothy Shaver, who could point to the sales figures at Lord & Taylor.\nCould utility alone justify the new ideas of the American designers? Fashion is often regarded as a pursuit of beauty, and some cherished fashion's trivial relationship to the fine arts. What the designers of the American sportswear proved was that fashion is a genuine design art, answering to the demanding needs of service. Of course these practical, insightful designers have determined the course of late twentieth-century fashion. They were the pioneers of gender equity, in their useful, adaptable clothing, which was both made for the masses and capable of self-expression.", "48": "We live today indebted to McCardell, Cashin, Hawes, Wilkins, and Maxwell, and other women who liberated American fashion from the confines of Parisian design. Independence came in tying, wrapping, storing, harmonizing, and rationalizing that wardrobe. These designers established the modern dress code, letting playsuits and other active wear outfits suffice for casual clothing, allowing pants to enter the wardrobe, and prizing rationalism and versatility in dress, in contradiction to dressing for an occasion or allotment of the day. Fashion in America was logical and answerable to the will of the women who wore it. Implicitly or explicitly, American fashion addressed a democracy, whereas traditional Paris-based fashion was prescriptive and imposed on women, willing or not.\nIn an earlier time, American fashion had also followed the dictates of Paris, or even copied and pirated specific French designs. Designer sportswear was not modeled on that of Europe, as \"modern art\" would later be; it was genuinely invented and developed in America. Its designers were not high-end with supplementary lines. The design objective and the business commitment were to sportswear, and the distinctive traits were problem-solving ingenuity and realistic lifestyle applications. Ease of care was most important: summer dresses and outfits, in particular, were chiefly cotton, readily capable of being washed and pressed at home. Closings were simple, practical, and accessible, as the modern woman depended on no personal maid to dress her. American designers prized resourcefulness and the freedom of women who wore the clothing.\nMany have argued that the women designers of this time were able to project their own clothing values into a new style. Of course, much of this argument in the 1930s-40s was advanced because there was little or no experience in justifying apparel(服装) on the basis of utility. If Paris was cast aside, the tradition of beauty was also to some degree slighted. Designer sportswear would have to be verified by a standard other than that of pure beauty; the emulation of a designer's life in designer sportswear was a crude version of this relationship. The consumer was ultimately to be mentioned as well, especially by the likes of Dorothy Shaver, who could point to the sales figures at Lord & Taylor.\nCould utility alone justify the new ideas of the American designers? Fashion is often regarded as a pursuit of beauty, and some cherished fashion's trivial relationship to the fine arts. What the designers of the American sportswear proved was that fashion is a genuine design art, answering to the demanding needs of service. Of course these practical, insightful designers have determined the course of late twentieth-century fashion. They were the pioneers of gender equity, in their useful, adaptable clothing, which was both made for the masses and capable of self-expression.", "49": "We live today indebted to McCardell, Cashin, Hawes, Wilkins, and Maxwell, and other women who liberated American fashion from the confines of Parisian design. Independence came in tying, wrapping, storing, harmonizing, and rationalizing that wardrobe. These designers established the modern dress code, letting playsuits and other active wear outfits suffice for casual clothing, allowing pants to enter the wardrobe, and prizing rationalism and versatility in dress, in contradiction to dressing for an occasion or allotment of the day. Fashion in America was logical and answerable to the will of the women who wore it. Implicitly or explicitly, American fashion addressed a democracy, whereas traditional Paris-based fashion was prescriptive and imposed on women, willing or not.\nIn an earlier time, American fashion had also followed the dictates of Paris, or even copied and pirated specific French designs. Designer sportswear was not modeled on that of Europe, as \"modern art\" would later be; it was genuinely invented and developed in America. Its designers were not high-end with supplementary lines. The design objective and the business commitment were to sportswear, and the distinctive traits were problem-solving ingenuity and realistic lifestyle applications. Ease of care was most important: summer dresses and outfits, in particular, were chiefly cotton, readily capable of being washed and pressed at home. Closings were simple, practical, and accessible, as the modern woman depended on no personal maid to dress her. American designers prized resourcefulness and the freedom of women who wore the clothing.\nMany have argued that the women designers of this time were able to project their own clothing values into a new style. Of course, much of this argument in the 1930s-40s was advanced because there was little or no experience in justifying apparel(服装) on the basis of utility. If Paris was cast aside, the tradition of beauty was also to some degree slighted. Designer sportswear would have to be verified by a standard other than that of pure beauty; the emulation of a designer's life in designer sportswear was a crude version of this relationship. The consumer was ultimately to be mentioned as well, especially by the likes of Dorothy Shaver, who could point to the sales figures at Lord & Taylor.\nCould utility alone justify the new ideas of the American designers? Fashion is often regarded as a pursuit of beauty, and some cherished fashion's trivial relationship to the fine arts. What the designers of the American sportswear proved was that fashion is a genuine design art, answering to the demanding needs of service. Of course these practical, insightful designers have determined the course of late twentieth-century fashion. They were the pioneers of gender equity, in their useful, adaptable clothing, which was both made for the masses and capable of self-expression.", "50": "We live today indebted to McCardell, Cashin, Hawes, Wilkins, and Maxwell, and other women who liberated American fashion from the confines of Parisian design. Independence came in tying, wrapping, storing, harmonizing, and rationalizing that wardrobe. These designers established the modern dress code, letting playsuits and other active wear outfits suffice for casual clothing, allowing pants to enter the wardrobe, and prizing rationalism and versatility in dress, in contradiction to dressing for an occasion or allotment of the day. Fashion in America was logical and answerable to the will of the women who wore it. Implicitly or explicitly, American fashion addressed a democracy, whereas traditional Paris-based fashion was prescriptive and imposed on women, willing or not.\nIn an earlier time, American fashion had also followed the dictates of Paris, or even copied and pirated specific French designs. Designer sportswear was not modeled on that of Europe, as \"modern art\" would later be; it was genuinely invented and developed in America. Its designers were not high-end with supplementary lines. The design objective and the business commitment were to sportswear, and the distinctive traits were problem-solving ingenuity and realistic lifestyle applications. Ease of care was most important: summer dresses and outfits, in particular, were chiefly cotton, readily capable of being washed and pressed at home. Closings were simple, practical, and accessible, as the modern woman depended on no personal maid to dress her. American designers prized resourcefulness and the freedom of women who wore the clothing.\nMany have argued that the women designers of this time were able to project their own clothing values into a new style. Of course, much of this argument in the 1930s-40s was advanced because there was little or no experience in justifying apparel(服装) on the basis of utility. If Paris was cast aside, the tradition of beauty was also to some degree slighted. Designer sportswear would have to be verified by a standard other than that of pure beauty; the emulation of a designer's life in designer sportswear was a crude version of this relationship. The consumer was ultimately to be mentioned as well, especially by the likes of Dorothy Shaver, who could point to the sales figures at Lord & Taylor.\nCould utility alone justify the new ideas of the American designers? Fashion is often regarded as a pursuit of beauty, and some cherished fashion's trivial relationship to the fine arts. What the designers of the American sportswear proved was that fashion is a genuine design art, answering to the demanding needs of service. Of course these practical, insightful designers have determined the course of late twentieth-century fashion. They were the pioneers of gender equity, in their useful, adaptable clothing, which was both made for the masses and capable of self-expression.", "51": "Massive rubbish dumps and sprawling landfills constitute one of the more uncomfortable impacts that humans have on wildlife. They have led some birds to give up on migration. Instead of flying thousands of miles in search of food, they make the waste sites their winter feeding grounds.\nResearchers in Germany used miniature GPS tags to track the migrations of 70 white storks(鹳) from different sites across Europe and Asia during the first five months of their lives. While many birds travelled along well-known routes to warmer climates, others stopped short and spent the winter on landfills, feeding on food waste, and the multitudes of insects that thrive on the dumps.\nIn the short-term, the birds seem to benefit from overwintering(过冬) on rubbish dumps. Andrea Flack of the Max Planck Institute found that birds following traditional migration routes were more likely to die than German storks that flew only as far as northern Morocco, and spent the winter there on rubbish dumps. \"For the birds it's a very convenient way to get food. There are huge clusters of organic waste they can feed on,\" said Flack. The meals are not particularly appetising, or even safe. Much of the waste is discarded rotten meat, mixed in with other human debris such as plastic bags and old toys.\n\"It's very risky. The birds can easily eat pieces of plastic or rubber bands and they can die,\" said Flack. \"And we don't know about the long-term consequences. They might eat something toxic and damage their health. We cannot estimate that yet.\"\nThe scientists tracked white storks from different colonies in Europe and Africa. The Russian, Greek and Polish storks flew as far as South Africa, while those from Spain, Tunisia and Germany flew only as far as the Sahel.\nLandfill sites on the Iberian peninsula have long attracted local white storks, but all of the Spanish birds tagged in the study flew across the Sahara desert to the western Sahel. Writing in the journal, the scientists describe how the storks from Germany were clearly affected by the presence of waste sites, with four out of six birds that survived for at least five months overwintering on rubbish dumps in northern Morocco, instead of migrating to the Sahel.\nFlack said it was too early to know whether the benefits of plentiful food outweighed the risks of feeding on landfills. But that's not the only uncertainty. Migrating birds affect ecosystems both at home and at their winter destinations, and disrupting the traditional routes could have unexpected side effects. White storks feed on locusts(蝗虫) and other insects that can become pests if their numbers get out of hand. \"They provide a useful service,\" said Flack.", "52": "Massive rubbish dumps and sprawling landfills constitute one of the more uncomfortable impacts that humans have on wildlife. They have led some birds to give up on migration. Instead of flying thousands of miles in search of food, they make the waste sites their winter feeding grounds.\nResearchers in Germany used miniature GPS tags to track the migrations of 70 white storks(鹳) from different sites across Europe and Asia during the first five months of their lives. While many birds travelled along well-known routes to warmer climates, others stopped short and spent the winter on landfills, feeding on food waste, and the multitudes of insects that thrive on the dumps.\nIn the short-term, the birds seem to benefit from overwintering(过冬) on rubbish dumps. Andrea Flack of the Max Planck Institute found that birds following traditional migration routes were more likely to die than German storks that flew only as far as northern Morocco, and spent the winter there on rubbish dumps. \"For the birds it's a very convenient way to get food. There are huge clusters of organic waste they can feed on,\" said Flack. The meals are not particularly appetising, or even safe. Much of the waste is discarded rotten meat, mixed in with other human debris such as plastic bags and old toys.\n\"It's very risky. The birds can easily eat pieces of plastic or rubber bands and they can die,\" said Flack. \"And we don't know about the long-term consequences. They might eat something toxic and damage their health. We cannot estimate that yet.\"\nThe scientists tracked white storks from different colonies in Europe and Africa. The Russian, Greek and Polish storks flew as far as South Africa, while those from Spain, Tunisia and Germany flew only as far as the Sahel.\nLandfill sites on the Iberian peninsula have long attracted local white storks, but all of the Spanish birds tagged in the study flew across the Sahara desert to the western Sahel. Writing in the journal, the scientists describe how the storks from Germany were clearly affected by the presence of waste sites, with four out of six birds that survived for at least five months overwintering on rubbish dumps in northern Morocco, instead of migrating to the Sahel.\nFlack said it was too early to know whether the benefits of plentiful food outweighed the risks of feeding on landfills. But that's not the only uncertainty. Migrating birds affect ecosystems both at home and at their winter destinations, and disrupting the traditional routes could have unexpected side effects. White storks feed on locusts(蝗虫) and other insects that can become pests if their numbers get out of hand. \"They provide a useful service,\" said Flack.", "53": "Massive rubbish dumps and sprawling landfills constitute one of the more uncomfortable impacts that humans have on wildlife. They have led some birds to give up on migration. Instead of flying thousands of miles in search of food, they make the waste sites their winter feeding grounds.\nResearchers in Germany used miniature GPS tags to track the migrations of 70 white storks(鹳) from different sites across Europe and Asia during the first five months of their lives. While many birds travelled along well-known routes to warmer climates, others stopped short and spent the winter on landfills, feeding on food waste, and the multitudes of insects that thrive on the dumps.\nIn the short-term, the birds seem to benefit from overwintering(过冬) on rubbish dumps. Andrea Flack of the Max Planck Institute found that birds following traditional migration routes were more likely to die than German storks that flew only as far as northern Morocco, and spent the winter there on rubbish dumps. \"For the birds it's a very convenient way to get food. There are huge clusters of organic waste they can feed on,\" said Flack. The meals are not particularly appetising, or even safe. Much of the waste is discarded rotten meat, mixed in with other human debris such as plastic bags and old toys.\n\"It's very risky. The birds can easily eat pieces of plastic or rubber bands and they can die,\" said Flack. \"And we don't know about the long-term consequences. They might eat something toxic and damage their health. We cannot estimate that yet.\"\nThe scientists tracked white storks from different colonies in Europe and Africa. The Russian, Greek and Polish storks flew as far as South Africa, while those from Spain, Tunisia and Germany flew only as far as the Sahel.\nLandfill sites on the Iberian peninsula have long attracted local white storks, but all of the Spanish birds tagged in the study flew across the Sahara desert to the western Sahel. Writing in the journal, the scientists describe how the storks from Germany were clearly affected by the presence of waste sites, with four out of six birds that survived for at least five months overwintering on rubbish dumps in northern Morocco, instead of migrating to the Sahel.\nFlack said it was too early to know whether the benefits of plentiful food outweighed the risks of feeding on landfills. But that's not the only uncertainty. Migrating birds affect ecosystems both at home and at their winter destinations, and disrupting the traditional routes could have unexpected side effects. White storks feed on locusts(蝗虫) and other insects that can become pests if their numbers get out of hand. \"They provide a useful service,\" said Flack.", "54": "Massive rubbish dumps and sprawling landfills constitute one of the more uncomfortable impacts that humans have on wildlife. They have led some birds to give up on migration. Instead of flying thousands of miles in search of food, they make the waste sites their winter feeding grounds.\nResearchers in Germany used miniature GPS tags to track the migrations of 70 white storks(鹳) from different sites across Europe and Asia during the first five months of their lives. While many birds travelled along well-known routes to warmer climates, others stopped short and spent the winter on landfills, feeding on food waste, and the multitudes of insects that thrive on the dumps.\nIn the short-term, the birds seem to benefit from overwintering(过冬) on rubbish dumps. Andrea Flack of the Max Planck Institute found that birds following traditional migration routes were more likely to die than German storks that flew only as far as northern Morocco, and spent the winter there on rubbish dumps. \"For the birds it's a very convenient way to get food. There are huge clusters of organic waste they can feed on,\" said Flack. The meals are not particularly appetising, or even safe. Much of the waste is discarded rotten meat, mixed in with other human debris such as plastic bags and old toys.\n\"It's very risky. The birds can easily eat pieces of plastic or rubber bands and they can die,\" said Flack. \"And we don't know about the long-term consequences. They might eat something toxic and damage their health. We cannot estimate that yet.\"\nThe scientists tracked white storks from different colonies in Europe and Africa. The Russian, Greek and Polish storks flew as far as South Africa, while those from Spain, Tunisia and Germany flew only as far as the Sahel.\nLandfill sites on the Iberian peninsula have long attracted local white storks, but all of the Spanish birds tagged in the study flew across the Sahara desert to the western Sahel. Writing in the journal, the scientists describe how the storks from Germany were clearly affected by the presence of waste sites, with four out of six birds that survived for at least five months overwintering on rubbish dumps in northern Morocco, instead of migrating to the Sahel.\nFlack said it was too early to know whether the benefits of plentiful food outweighed the risks of feeding on landfills. But that's not the only uncertainty. Migrating birds affect ecosystems both at home and at their winter destinations, and disrupting the traditional routes could have unexpected side effects. White storks feed on locusts(蝗虫) and other insects that can become pests if their numbers get out of hand. \"They provide a useful service,\" said Flack.", "55": "Massive rubbish dumps and sprawling landfills constitute one of the more uncomfortable impacts that humans have on wildlife. They have led some birds to give up on migration. Instead of flying thousands of miles in search of food, they make the waste sites their winter feeding grounds.\nResearchers in Germany used miniature GPS tags to track the migrations of 70 white storks(鹳) from different sites across Europe and Asia during the first five months of their lives. While many birds travelled along well-known routes to warmer climates, others stopped short and spent the winter on landfills, feeding on food waste, and the multitudes of insects that thrive on the dumps.\nIn the short-term, the birds seem to benefit from overwintering(过冬) on rubbish dumps. Andrea Flack of the Max Planck Institute found that birds following traditional migration routes were more likely to die than German storks that flew only as far as northern Morocco, and spent the winter there on rubbish dumps. \"For the birds it's a very convenient way to get food. There are huge clusters of organic waste they can feed on,\" said Flack. The meals are not particularly appetising, or even safe. Much of the waste is discarded rotten meat, mixed in with other human debris such as plastic bags and old toys.\n\"It's very risky. The birds can easily eat pieces of plastic or rubber bands and they can die,\" said Flack. \"And we don't know about the long-term consequences. They might eat something toxic and damage their health. We cannot estimate that yet.\"\nThe scientists tracked white storks from different colonies in Europe and Africa. The Russian, Greek and Polish storks flew as far as South Africa, while those from Spain, Tunisia and Germany flew only as far as the Sahel.\nLandfill sites on the Iberian peninsula have long attracted local white storks, but all of the Spanish birds tagged in the study flew across the Sahara desert to the western Sahel. Writing in the journal, the scientists describe how the storks from Germany were clearly affected by the presence of waste sites, with four out of six birds that survived for at least five months overwintering on rubbish dumps in northern Morocco, instead of migrating to the Sahel.\nFlack said it was too early to know whether the benefits of plentiful food outweighed the risks of feeding on landfills. But that's not the only uncertainty. Migrating birds affect ecosystems both at home and at their winter destinations, and disrupting the traditional routes could have unexpected side effects. White storks feed on locusts(蝗虫) and other insects that can become pests if their numbers get out of hand. \"They provide a useful service,\" said Flack."}, "questions": [{"id": "2017-06-2-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "How would the man feel if he found his book in a second-hand bookstore?", "options": ["A) He would feel insulted.", "B) He would feel very sad.", "C) He would be embarrassed.", "D) He would be disappointed."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度观点题） He would feel insulted.\n【定位】If they've got my book there, I think, \"Well, this is an insult! Somebody didn't want to keep\nmy book!\"\n如果他们那儿有我的书，我会想：\"嗯，这是一种侮辱！有人不想留着我的书！\"\n【信号】被问者的直接应答。女主持问到二手书店见到自己的书，男方（被采访者）开口应答 this is\nan insult，被问者的这句直接回应就是答案落点，听力对话题的答案几乎总在被问的那个人\n嘴里。\n【替换】this is an insult ↔ He would feel insulted.\n名词转形容词\n【排除】B)\nHe would feel very sad. 听上去是合理的负面情绪，但录音里他说的是 this is an insult\n（被侮辱），从未提到 sad，是把\"被侮辱\"想当然换成了\"悲伤\"。\nC)\nHe would be embarrassed. 的\"尴尬\"看似贴合\"在二手店见到自己的书\"的场景，但录音里\n他给的反应是 insult 而非 embarrassed，原文并无此情绪。\nD)\nHe would be disappointed. 的\"失望\"同样是另起的情绪，录音中他的反应是被侮辱\n（insult），并未说失望，属于另换情绪的干扰。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What does the man's wife think of his books?", "options": ["A) They are worthy of a prize.", "B) They are of little value.", "C) They make good reading.", "D) They need improvement."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） They need improvement.\n【定位】I began working on my latest book, The Buried Giant, in 2004 but I stopped after I showed\nmy wife a little section. She thought it was rubbish.\n我2004年开始写我最新的书《被掩埋的巨人》，但给妻子看了一小段后就停下了。她觉得那\n是垃圾。\n【信号】妻子的评价用词。男方转述妻子的看法时落在 She thought it was rubbish，rubbish 是带\n强烈贬义的评价词，这种评判性用词就是\"问看法\"题的答案信号。\n【替换】She thought it was rubbish ↔ They need improvement.\n贬义概括为需改进\n【排除】A)\nThey are worthy of a prize. 是高度肯定，与妻子 rubbish 的强烈否定恰好相反；题中虽提\n到 Booker Prize，但那是他获过奖、并非妻子认为书\"配得奖\"，属把否定听成肯定。\n借用原文：She thought it was rubbish\nB)\nThey are of little value.（几乎没价值）方向虽是贬义，但等于全盘否定整本书的价值；妻\n子批评的其实只是 It's always about execution.（写法、执行），并非说书毫无价值，程\n度被夸大。\n借用原文：It's always about execution.\nC)\nThey make good reading.（很好读、好看）是正面评价，与妻子说的 rubbish 完全相\n反，是把否定评价反向理解。\n借用原文：She thought it was rubbish", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What does the man do when he engages in writing?", "options": ["A) He seldom writes a book straight through.", "B) He writes several books simultaneously.", "C) He draws on his real-life experiences.", "D) He often turns to his wife for help."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） He seldom writes a book straight through.\n【定位】I've often stopped writing a book and left it for a few years. And by the time I come back to it,\nit may have changed.\n我常常会停下一本书的写作，把它搁置好几年。等我再回头去写时，它可能已经变了。\n【信号】频率词 often 领出习惯。他用 I've often stopped writing a book and left it for a few\nyears，频率词 often＋\"停笔搁置几年\"直接交代了写作习惯，这种描述常态做法的句子就是\n本题答案句。\n【替换】I've often stopped writing a book and left it for a few years ↔\nHe seldom writes a book straight through.\n肯否转换说同一事\n【排除】B)\nHe writes several books simultaneously. 像是对\"停一本写别的\"的合理联想，但录音只说\n他停下一本、搁置几年再回来 left it for a few years，从没说同时写好几本，是凭印象多推\n一步。\n借用原文：left it for a few years\nC)\nHe draws on his real-life experiences. 听起来很像作家常态，但录音里他谈的是\"停笔、\n搁置、回来再改\"的流程，全程没提从真实生活取材，属未提及。\nD) 原词陷阱\nHe often turns to his wife for help. 盗用了前文 my wife 这个原词，但妻子在文中是批评\n他写法（rubbish）的人，不是他写作时求助的对象，把\"妻子批评\"偷换成\"向妻子求助\"。\n借用原文：I showed my wife a little section", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What does the man want to say by mentioning the football match?", "options": ["A) Writing a book is just like watching a football match.", "B) Writers actually work every bit as hard as footballers.", "C) He likes watching a football match after finishing a book.", "D) Unlike a football match, there is no end to writing a book."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （推断题） Unlike a football match, there is no end to writing a book.\n【定位】I watch footballers at the end of the match... at that moment they know it's over. It's funny for\nan author. There's never a finishing whistle.\n我看球员在比赛结束时……那一刻他们知道结束了。对作家来说就很奇怪。（写作）从来没\n有一声终场哨。\n【信号】对比后的落脚句。他先讲球员有终场哨、知道比赛结束，随即用 It's funny for an author.\nThere's never a finishing whistle. 把话锋转到作家身上——这句对比后的落脚句才是他真\n正想说的，是答案信号。\n【替换】There's never a finishing whistle ↔ Unlike a football match, there is no end to writing a book.\n形象说法转直白\nIt's funny for an author ↔ Unlike a football match\n对比关系对位\n【排除】A)\nWriting a book is just like watching a football match. 盗用了 football match 原词，但\n他的本意恰是\"写书与球赛不同\"（没有终场哨），说成\"就像看球赛\"把对比关系弄反了。\n借用原文：It's funny for an author\nB)\nWriters actually work every bit as hard as footballers. 借了 footballers 原词，并把话题\n引到\"作家和球员一样辛苦\"；但他对比的是\"有没有终点\"，根本没谈谁更辛苦，属另起话\n题。\n借用原文：I watch footballers at the end of the match\nC)\nHe likes watching a football match after finishing a book. 盗用 watch／football\nmatch 原词，编出\"写完书爱看球\"的个人爱好；录音里 watch footballers 只是打比方，他\n并未表达\"完书后喜欢看球\"。\n借用原文：I watch footballers at the end of the match", "points": ["听力·长对话", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What are the speakers talking about?", "options": ["A) Achievements of black male athletes in college.", "B) Financial assistance to black athletes in college.", "C) High college dropout rates among black athletes.", "D) Undergraduate enrollments of black athletes."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （主旨题） High college dropout rates among black athletes.\n【定位】According to a study of Race & Equity in Education, black athletes are dropping out of\ncollege across the country at alarming rates.\n根据一项《教育中的种族与公平》研究，全国各地的黑人运动员正以惊人的比例从大学\n辍学。\n【信号】开头点题句。主持人开场第一句 black athletes are dropping out of college across the\ncountry at alarming rates 直接抛出全场议题——「黑人运动员高辍学率」，这是长对话\n最常见的「开头点题」信号，主旨就藏在起话题的第一句。\n【替换】dropping out of college across the country at alarming rates ↔\nHigh college dropout rates among black athletes\n动词短语转名词短语\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nAchievements（成就）是正向，而全段讲的是辍学、拿不到学位的负面问题，方向完全相\n反，全程无人谈他们的「成就」。\nB) 原词陷阱\nFinancial assistance（经济资助）借了后文 revenue-generating、economic pressure 等\n「钱」相关词制造耳熟，但全段从未谈「给运动员经济资助」，只谈他们替学校挣钱、却毕\n不了业。\n借用原文：revenue-generating sports teams\nD) 原词陷阱\nUndergraduate enrollments 直接盗用录音原词 undergraduate enrollments，听到原词\n极易误选；但招生人数只是第 4 段用来对比的一个数据，不是全场主题，全场主题是「辍\n学/毕不了业」。\n借用原文：black males make up less than 3 percent of undergraduate enrollments", "points": ["听力·长对话", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What is the new finding about black male athletes in this study?", "options": ["A) They display great talent in every kind of game.", "B) They are better at sports than at academic work.", "C) They have difficulty finding money to complete their studies.", "D) They make money for the college but often fail to earn a degree."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They make money for the college but often fail to earn a degree.\n【定位】Yet, when you look at their numbers or percentages on the revenue-generating sports teams of\nfootball and basketball, they make up well into 50-60 percent of those teams. So the idea is\nthat they are really there to be part of the revenue-generating working class of athletes on\ncampus and not necessarily there to be part of the educating class.\n然而，看他们在足球、篮球这些创收球队上的人数或比例，却高达那些球队的 50-60%。所\n以这个意思是，他们其实是被当作校园里创收的「劳动者」运动员，而不一定是像其他多数\n学生那样属于受教育的群体。\n【信号】转折 Yet 后是落点。被问方先说招生占比不到 3%，紧接一个转折信号 Yet，转折之后才是\n研究的真正发现——他们在创收球队占 50-60%、是来「挣钱」而非「受教育」的。转折后\n那部分才是答案落点。\n【替换】revenue-generating sports teams ↔ make money for the college\n术语转大白话\nnot necessarily there to be part of the educating class ↔ often fail to earn a degree\n含蓄表达转明确结果\n【排除】A) 未提及\ndisplay great talent in every kind of game（在每种比赛都极有天赋）录音从未说，原文\n只提足球、篮球两类创收项目，every kind（每种）属夸大杜撰。\nB) 偷换概念\nbetter at sports than at academic work（更擅长运动而非学业）是常识刻板印象，但录\n音说的是制度把他们当「劳动者」用、没给他们受教育机会，并非他们「学习能力差」，归\n因被偷换。\nC) 原词陷阱\ndifficulty finding money to complete their studies（凑不出钱读完）借了录音的「钱」\n氛围（revenue、money）制造耳熟，但录音从没说他们缺钱读书，恰恰相反——是他们替\n学校挣钱。盗词却把因果方向弄反。\n借用原文：revenue-generating working class of athletes", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What is the graduation rate of black male athletes?", "options": ["A) About 15%.", "B) Around 40%.", "C) Slightly over 50%.", "D) Approximately 70%."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Slightly over 50%.\n【定位】Compared with other groups, I think the numbers in this group at those 65 schools are\nsomething like just barely more than half of the black male athletes graduate at all.\n和其他群体相比，我觉得这个群体在那 65 所学校的数字大概是——只有勉强过半的黑人男\n运动员最终能毕业。\n【信号】graduate 旁的比例词。答案数字不是直接念出百分号，而是用文字描述 just barely more\nthan half ... graduate（勉强过半才毕业）。锁定的信号是「比例词紧贴 graduate（毕\n业）」——题干问毕业率，就抓 graduate 旁边那个量。\n【替换】just barely more than half ↔ Slightly over 50%\n文字比例转百分数\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nAbout 15% 没有原文依据，是用一个偏小的数迷惑；录音里偏小的数是 less than 3\npercent（招生占比），但那不是毕业率，A 既非原数也非所问。\nB) 与原文相反\nAround 40% 把「勉强过半」往下挪到四成，just barely more than half 明确是「过\n半」，40% 是没过半，与原文程度不符。\n借用原文：barely more than half\nD) 原词陷阱\nApproximately 70% 把毕业率抬到七成，与「勉强过半」相差甚远；录音里偏高的数是\n50-60 percent（球队占比），D 既非该数也答非所问。\n借用原文：50-60 percent of those teams", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What accounts for black athletes' failure to obtain a college degree according to the man?", "options": ["A) Coaches lack the incentive to graduate them.", "B) College degrees do not count much to them.", "C) They have little interest in academic work.", "D) Schools do not deem it a serious problem."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Coaches lack the incentive to graduate them.\n【定位】Well, I think one of the reasons it hasn't changed is because there is really no economic\npressure to change this. All of the incentive is really on winning and not losing on the field or\non the court. Coaches do not necessarily have the incentive to graduate players.\n嗯，我觉得它之所以没改变，原因之一是确实没有经济上的压力去改变它。所有的激励都在\n赛场上赢球、别输球，教练并不一定有让球员毕业的动力。\n【信号】one of the reasons...because。男士回答 why 时连用因果信号 one of the reasons it\nhasn't changed is because，明确引出原因；其后 Coaches do not necessarily have the\nincentive to graduate players 把责任落到「教练缺乏让球员毕业的动力」上，这是答案\n落点。\n【替换】Coaches do not necessarily have the incentive to graduate players ↔\nCoaches lack the incentive to graduate them\n近乎原句的同义改写\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\nCollege degrees do not count much to them（学位对他们不值钱）录音从未说；男士恰\n恰强调学位是 a transformative tool（能改变命运的工具），即学位很重要，与 B 相反。\n借用原文：a college degree, which can be a transformative tool\nC) 偷换概念\nThey have little interest in academic work（学生对学业没兴趣）是最符合刻板印象、最\n易误选的项，但男士给的原因是「制度上没有经济压力、教练没动力」，归因在制度/教\n练，不在学生主观意愿。\nD) 张冠李戴\nSchools do not deem it a serious problem（学校不当回事）盗用了录音 economic\npressure、incentive 的「制度」氛围，但男士把责任明确落在 coaches（教练），不是\nschools（学校），把对象换了人。\n借用原文：Coaches do not necessarily have the incentive to graduate players", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What is the speaker mainly talking about?", "options": ["A) Marketing strategies.", "B) Holiday shopping.", "C) Shopping malls.", "D) Online stores."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （主旨题） Holiday shopping.\n【定位】America's holiday shopping season starts on Black Friday, the day after Thanksgiving. It is\nthe busiest shopping day of the year.\n美国的节日购物季从黑色星期五开始，即感恩节后的第二天。这是一年中最繁忙的购物日。\n【信号】开头点题句。录音第一句 America's holiday shopping season starts on Black Friday 开\n门见山点出 holiday shopping season，是篇章独白典型的开头点题；随后全文从黑五、网\n络星期一到商场、消费，始终绕着节日购物展开，主旨即在此。\n【替换】America's holiday shopping season ↔ Holiday shopping.\n原话即话题词\n【排除】A) 未提及\nMarketing strategies. 营销策略，录音通篇讲消费数据与商场处境，从未谈商家如何营\n销，属凭话题想当然添加的内容。\nC) 原词陷阱\nShopping malls. 只是第三段重点谈的一个侧面，录音确实念到 traditional shopping\nmalls，但它罩不住前两段的购物季与网购，拿局部当主旨。\nD) 原词陷阱\nOnline stores. 网店只是第二段的话题，录音说 More and more will shop online，但它\n同样只覆盖一段，无法概括全篇的节日购物主题。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "How many people will shop on Cyber Monday?", "options": ["A) About 50% of holiday shoppers.", "B) About 20-30% of holiday shoppers.", "C) About 136 million.", "D) About 183.8 million."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） About 183.8 million.\n【定位】About 183.8 million people will shop on Cyber Monday, the first Monday\nafter Thanksgiving.\n大约有1.838亿人会在网络星期一购物，即感恩节后的第一个星期一。\n【信号】数字紧贴对象词。答案句把数字直接系在对象上：183.8 million people will shop on\nCyber Monday，数值 183.8 million 与题干所问的 Cyber Monday 同句出现，这种数字\n加专有时点的搭配就是定位信号。\n【替换】183.8 million people will shop on Cyber Monday ↔ About 183.8 million.\n数字精确对应\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nAbout 50% of holiday shoppers. 借了录音里 More than half of all holiday purchases\n（超过一半网购）的一半之意，但那说的是网购占比，不是 Cyber Monday 的购物人数，\n量纲都不对。\nB) 原词陷阱\nAbout 20-30% of holiday shoppers. 盗用第一段 about 20 to 30 percent of annual\nrevenue 的数字，但那是零售商的年收入占比，既非人数也不属 Cyber Monday，安错了\n对象。\nC) 原词陷阱\nAbout 136 million. 取自第一段 About 136 million people will shop during the\nThanksgiving Holiday weekend，是感恩节周末的人数，不是题干所问的 Cyber\nMonday，对象错位。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What does Fortune say about traditional shopping malls?", "options": ["A) They have fewer customers.", "B) They find it hard to survive.", "C) They are thriving once more.", "D) They appeal to elderly customers."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They are thriving once more.\n【定位】However, Fortune says the weakest of the malls have closed. The sector is thriving again.\n然而，《财富》杂志称，最弱的那些商场已经关门，该行业正在重新繁荣。\n【信号】转折However加出处。答案句以 However 起头，扭转前文 more than 24 shopping\nmalls have closed 的衰败叙述，紧跟出处 Fortune says 后给出结论 the sector is\nthriving again——转折词加点名出处，正是答案句的双重信号。\n【替换】The sector is thriving again. ↔ They are thriving once more.\nagain等于once more\n【排除】A) 未提及\nThey have fewer customers. 顾客减少，录音并未说 Fortune 这样讲，且与转折后的\nthriving 结论矛盾，是按对网购冲击的印象想当然填的。\nB) 与原文相反\nThey find it hard to survive. 难以生存，对应的是转折前 an additional 60 are\nstruggling 的困境描述，但那不是 Fortune 的结论，Fortune 说的是 However 之后的回\n暖。\nD) 未提及\nThey appeal to elderly customers. 吸引老年顾客，录音通篇没提任何年龄群体，是凭空\n添加的内容。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What is said to account for the increased number of shoppers?", "options": ["A) Better quality of consumer goods.", "B) Higher employment and wages.", "C) Greater varieties of commodities.", "D) People having more leisure time."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Higher employment and wages.\n【定位】Economist Gus Faucher said lower unemployment and rising wages could give Americans\nmore money to spend.\n经济学家古斯·福彻表示，失业率下降和工资上涨可能让美国人有更多的钱可花。\n【信号】could give因果句。答案句由经济学家给出，含因果结构 could give Americans more\nmoney to spend，把 lower unemployment and rising wages 设为让人有更多钱花、从\n而多购物的原因，could give 就是因果信号。\n【替换】lower unemployment and rising wages ↔ Higher employment and wages.\n失业降即就业升\n【排除】A) 未提及\nBetter quality of consumer goods. 商品质量更好，听上去像合理购物理由，但录音的因\n果句只谈就业与工资，从未提商品质量。\nC) 未提及\nGreater varieties of commodities. 商品种类更多，同样是个看似合理的购物诱因，但录音\n因果句里没有这层意思。\nD) 未提及\nPeople having more leisure time. 闲暇变多，是关于消费增加最常见的想当然解释，但录\n音明确把原因归于就业和工资，未提闲暇。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What do we learn about the \"superbugs\"?", "options": ["A) They are new species of big insects.", "B) They are overprescribed antibiotics.", "C) They are life-threatening diseases.", "D) They are antibiotic-resistant bacteria."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They are antibiotic-resistant bacteria.\n【定位】In fact, you may have heard about the new \"superbugs\", which are antibiotic-resistant bacteria\nthat have developed as a result of overprescribed antibiotics.\n事实上，你可能听说过新出现的\"超级细菌\"，它们就是因抗生素被过度开具而产生的耐药\n细菌。\n【信号】which are 定义结构。答案信号是定语从句的定义结构 which are antibiotic-resistant\nbacteria——superbugs 后面紧跟 which are，直接给出它的身份，这种'名词 + which are\n+ 解释'就是定义题的落点。\n【替换】which are antibiotic-resistant bacteria ↔ They are antibiotic-resistant bacteria.\n定语从句改写为独立陈述句\n【排除】A) 未提及\nThey are new species of big insects. 把 superbugs 里的 bug 望文生义当成昆虫，但录\n音明确说是 antibiotic-resistant bacteria（细菌），从未提'昆虫/新物种'，纯属对 bug 一\n词的误解。\nB) 原词陷阱\nThey are overprescribed antibiotics. 借用了答案句里的原词 overprescribed\nantibiotics，听到这个词很想选；但录音说耐药菌是'因抗生素被过度开具 as a result of\noverprescribed antibiotics'而产生，过度开具是原因，superbugs 本身是细菌、不是'被过\n度开具的抗生素'，混淆了原因与对象。\n借用原文：as a result of overprescribed antibiotics\nC) 偷换概念\nThey are life-threatening diseases. 借了录音里的原词 life-threatening situations，但录\n音说的是吃抗生素可能带来'威胁生命的处境'，而 superbugs 本身是细菌，不是'危及生命\n的疾病'；细菌与疾病是两个概念，被偷换了。\n借用原文：life-threatening situations", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What is the result of the overuse of antibiotics?", "options": ["A) Antibiotics are now in short supply.", "B) Many infections are no longer curable.", "C) Large amounts of tax money are wasted.", "D) Routine operations have become complex."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Routine operations have become complex.\n【定位】And, apparently, that day has come because seemingly routine operations such as knee\nreplacements are now much more hazardous due to the looming threat of these infections.\n而且，那一天显然已经到来，因为像膝关节置换这类看似常规的手术，如今由于这些感染步\n步逼近的威胁而变得危险得多。\n【信号】now 表前后变化的结果。答案信号是表状态变化的 routine operations such as knee\nreplacements are now much more hazardous 中的 now（如今……）——它点出常规手\n术'现在'变得更危险，这种'now + 变化'正是抗生素滥用带来的结果落点。\n【替换】seemingly routine operations such as knee replacements are now much more hazardous ↔\nRoutine operations have become complex.\ncommon/routine 操作的负面变化对接\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nAntibiotics are now in short supply. 说抗生素供应短缺，但录音通篇讲的是抗生素被'过\n度使用'导致耐药，从未提供应量不足；'短缺'与'滥用'方向恰恰相反。\n借用原文：overprescribed antibiotics\nB) 过度推断\nMany infections are no longer curable. 借了录音里的原词 these infections，听感很\n贴；但录音只说提供医疗变得'很困难 very difficult'、手术更危险，并未说'许多感染再也无\n法治愈 no longer curable'，把'更难治'夸大成了'治不了'。\n借用原文：it would become very difficult to provide medical care\nC) 答非所问\nLarge amounts of tax money are wasted. 听起来像录音末段的钱事，但末段说的是研究\n经费'投入不足 only 1.2 percent'，是'钱花得太少'，并非'税款被浪费'；而且那是讲'解决问\n题需要什么'，不是'滥用抗生素的结果'，把第15题的内容错接到本题上。\n借用原文：only 1.2 percent of budgetary money", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What is most urgently needed for tackling the large life-threatening epidemic, according to the speaker?", "options": ["A) Facilities.", "B) Expertise.", "C) Money.", "D) Publicity."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Money.\n【定位】With such a large life-threatening epidemic, it is sad to say that only 1.2 percent of budgetary\nmoney for the National Institutes of Health is currently being spent on research to tackle this\nproblem. This is a far cry from the funds necessary for a problem of such magnitude.\n面对如此大规模、危及生命的流行病，遗憾的是，美国国立卫生研究院目前只把1.2%的预算\n用于研究以应对这一问题。这与解决如此严重问题所需的资金相差甚远。\n【信号】only + a far cry 表资金严重不足。答案信号是表'严重不足'的两处：only 1.2 percent of\nbudgetary money 的 only（仅仅1.2%）与 This is a far cry from the funds necessary\n的 a far cry from（相差甚远）；既然钱被反复说成远远不够，那'最迫切需要的'自然就\n是钱。\n【替换】the funds necessary for a problem of such magnitude ↔ Money\nfunds 上位概括为 Money\n【排除】A) 未提及\nFacilities（设施）听起来像医疗领域常需的资源，但录音谈的全是研究'经费/资金'不足，从\n未提到设施或场地短缺，属凭话题联想硬填。\nB) 未提及\nExpertise（专业知识/人才）和资金同属'解决难题所需资源'、最易混；但录音只点出 only", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What does the speaker say about a university?", "options": ["A) It is accessible only to the talented.", "B) It improves students' ability to think.", "C) It starts a lifelong learning process.", "D) It gives birth to many eminent scholars."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It improves students' ability to think.\n【定位】You are here to understand thinking better and to think better yourself. ... For the next few\nyears, you have a chance to focus on thinking.\n你来这里是为了更好地理解思考，也让自己思考得更好。……在接下来的几年里，你有机会\n专注于思考。\n【信号】反复强调 thinking。开篇连续出现 to understand thinking better and to think better\nyourself 与 a chance to focus on thinking，同一个词被反复强调，正是说话人对大学价\n值的核心定调，这种重复就是答案信号。\n【替换】to understand thinking better and to think better yourself ↔ improves students' ability to think\n动词化概括同义\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音说你们来这里就是为了受教育，面向全体学生，从未说大学只对有天赋的人开放，only\nto the talented 的 only 是凭空加上的范围限制。\nC) 与原文相反\n选项说大学开启终身学习，但录音明确讲 It isn't a chance you're going to have\nthroughout your lifetime.——这是一生中难得、并非贯穿终身的机会，C 恰好说反了。\n借用原文：It isn't a chance you're going to have throughout your lifetime.\nD) 过度推断\n后文确实提到一位学生今日成了被引用最多的经济学家，但那是单个例子；It gives birth to\nmany eminent scholars. 把个例夸大成大学批量培养名家，并非说话人对大学的定调。\n借用原文：his works are more cited as an economist than any other economist in the world", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What do we learn from the speaker's stories about universities?", "options": ["A) They encourage academic democracy.", "B) They promote globalization.", "C) They uphold the presidents' authority.", "D) They protect students' rights."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They encourage academic democracy.\n【定位】what was really important about that was the universities stand out as places that really are\nabout the authority of ideas. You see it in faculty members who are pleased when their\nstudents make a discovery that undermines a cherished theory that they had put forward.\n这件事真正重要的地方在于，大学之所以与众不同，正因为它讲的是「思想的权威」。你能\n在那些老师身上看到这一点：当学生的发现推翻了他们曾珍视并提出的理论时，他们反而感\n到高兴。\n【信号】what was really important 点题。what was really important about that was 是说话\n人把故事拔高成结论的强调句，紧随其后的 the authority of ideas 就是他要归纳的大学特\n点，这种「真正重要的是…」信号词后面才是落点。\n【替换】the authority of ideas ↔ academic democracy\n概念同义转写\nstudents make a discovery that undermines a cherished theory ↔ encourage academic democracy\n具体场景印证抽象词\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音里说话人确实讲过开了一门关于全球化的研讨课 I taught a seminar on\nglobalization，但那只是故事背景，本题归纳的特点是思想的权威，与推动全球化无关，B\n是听到 globalization 就想选的陷阱。\n借用原文：I taught a seminar on globalization\nC) 与原文相反\n选项说大学维护校长权威，但故事恰恰是大一新生当面指出校长论文站不住脚、说话人还大\n加赞赏，强调的是 the authority of ideas（思想的权威）而非头衔权威，C 把核心说反\n了。\n借用原文：the authority of ideas\nD) 未提及\n保护学生权益（rights）在整段故事里从未出现，录音谈的是学生敢质疑、老师乐于被推\n翻，与权益保护无关，They protect students' rights. 属凭空添加。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What does the speaker see in the young man who challenged his paper?", "options": ["A) His thirst for knowledge.", "B) His eagerness to find a job.", "C) His contempt for authority.", "D) His potential for leadership."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） His thirst for knowledge.\n【定位】You could debate whether they actually were mistakes, but you couldn't debate that young\nman's hunger to learn.\n你可以争论那些到底是不是错误，但你无法否认那个年轻人对学习的渴望。\n【信号】but 后的强调否定句。but you couldn't debate that young man's hunger to learn 用\n「你无法否认…」这一转折后的强调句，把说话人真正看重的品质拎了出来，but +\ncouldn't debate 就是答案信号，前半句只是铺垫。\n【替换】that young man's hunger to learn ↔ His thirst for knowledge.\n比喻同义替换\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n年轻人确实说过 he wanted a job，B 的 find a job 就盗用了这个原词；但说话人看重的是\n他求学的渴望，要工作只是表面情节，并非说话人「看到」的品质，属听到原词就想选的陷\n阱。\n借用原文：he wanted a job\nC) 与原文相反\n年轻人指出论文有错、敢于挑战，但说话人对此是欣赏（赞其求学之渴），并非说他蔑视权\n威；His contempt for authority. 把敢质疑曲解成贬义的蔑视，与说话人态度相反。\n借用原文：you couldn't debate that young man's hunger to learn\nD) 未提及\n说话人夸的是这个年轻人的求学渴望，以及他后来成了被引用最多的经济学家，从未提到领\n导潜质，His potential for leadership. 在录音里没有依据。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What does the simple test suggest?", "options": ["A) Few people know how to retrieve information properly.", "B) People can enhance their memory with a few tricks.", "C) Most people have a rather poor long-term memory.", "D) People tend to underestimate their mental powers."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （主旨题） People tend to underestimate their mental powers.\n【定位】Psychological research shows we consistently underestimate our mental powers.\n心理学研究表明,我们一贯低估了自己的心智能力。\n【信号】开篇论点句即结论。篇章一上来就甩出 Psychological research shows we consistently\nunderestimate our mental powers.,这是\"研究表明\"领出的开篇论点句,后面整段的测试都\n是为它服务的例证,所以这句就是承载结论的答案句。\n【替换】we consistently underestimate our mental powers ↔\nPeople tend to underestimate their mental powers\nwe→People、consistently→tend to,近乎同义\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 用了录音结尾的 your ability to retrieve information 这个原词组,可它讲的是\"记不起的\n原因是提取能力差\",而题问的是测试整体\"说明了什么\";A 把一个解释成因的细节冒充成总结\n论,且\"Few people know how\"是录音没说的程度判断,答非所问。\n借用原文：just your ability to retrieve information from it\nB) 答非所问\nB 说\"靠技巧能提升记忆\"确是整篇后半段(第2-4段)的主张,但第19题只问\"the simple\ntest\"这一个测试说明什么;测试本身得出的是\"低估脑力\",而非\"有技巧能提升\",B 把全篇后半\n的话题硬塞给这道只问测试的题,范围错位。\nC) 与原文相反\nC 说\"大多数人长期记忆很差\",而录音明说 it is not really your memory letting you\ndown(并不是你的记忆出问题),恰恰否定了\"记忆差\"这一说;C 取的是和讲者结论相反的极\n性。\n借用原文：it is not really your memory letting you down", "points": ["听力·讲座", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What do we learn about the two lists in the test?", "options": ["A) They present the states in a surprisingly different order.", "B) They include more or less the same number of states.", "C) They are exactly the same as is shown in the atlas.", "D) They contain names of the most familiar states."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They include more or less the same number of states.\n【定位】The two lists will contain roughly the same number of states, but they will not be identical.\n两份清单所含的州数大致相同,但它们并不完全一样。\n【信号】directly 描述 two lists 的对照句。录音里唯一正面描述 two lists 的就是 The two lists\nwill contain roughly the same number of states, but they will not be identical.,它直接\n给出两份清单的对照特征,且带 but 的对照结构,是承载本题答案的那一句。\n【替换】roughly the same number of states ↔ more or less the same number of states\nroughly=more or less,程度副词同义替换\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nA 说两份清单\"顺序惊人地不同\",borrow 了录音 something rather surprising\n的\"surprising\"气氛,可录音的对照点是\"数量大致相同、但不完全一样(换了几个州名)\",从没\n说\"顺序(order)\"不同;A 把维度从\"数量/内容\"偷换成\"顺序\"。\n借用原文：you will notice something rather surprising\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 搬来录音里的 atlas 一词,但录音说的是\"只要你没查 atlas\",并未拿清单和 atlas 比;而且录\n音明说两份清单 they will not be identical(并不完全相同),C 却说\"exactly the same\",绝对\n词且极性正好相反。\n借用原文：Provided you have not cheated by consulting an atlas\nD) 未提及\nD 说两份清单含\"最熟悉的州名(most familiar states)\",录音通篇没有按\"熟悉程度\"评价过哪\n些州名,只说数量相近、内容有出入;\"most familiar\"是录音从未出现的信息。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What does the speaker suggest about preparing for and taking an exam?", "options": ["A) Focusing on what is likely to be tested.", "B) Having a good sleep the night before.", "C) Reviewing your lessons where the exam is to take place.", "D) Making sensible decisions while choosing your answers."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Reviewing your lessons where the exam is to take place.\n【定位】If possible, you should also try to learn information in the room where it is going to be tested.\n如果可能的话,你还应当尽量在将要考试的那个房间里学习信息。\n【信号】建议信号 you should 领出的做法。本段两处建议中,落到题面的是 you should also try to\nlearn information in the room where it is going to be tested,由建议信号 you should\n领出,明确推荐\"在考试的房间里学\",这就是承载答案的建议句。\n【替换】learn information in the room where it is going to be tested ↔\nReviewing your lessons where the exam is to take place\nlearn→Reviewing、room…tested→where exam take place,同义改写\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA\"押可能考的内容\"借了录音里 tested 这个原词的余味,但录音从没建议过\"focus on what\nis likely to be tested(押重点)\";它只说在考试的房间里学、保持同样状态,A 把一个常识性的\n备考说法安到录音头上。\n借用原文：learn information in the room where it is going to be tested\nB) 未提及\nB\"考前一晚睡个好觉\"是极顺常识的备考建议,但录音第2段讲的是\"保持和学习时相同的状态\n与地点(如喝杯咖啡、回到考场房间)\",通篇没提到 sleep;B 纯属考生用常识脑补。\nD) 原词陷阱\nD\"答题时做明智的决定\"借了录音 it would be sensible 里的 sensible 一词,可那个\nsensible 修饰的是\"考前喝杯咖啡\"这件事,而非\"答题时如何抉择\";录音根本没讲考场上怎么\n选答案,D 把原词嫁接到一个录音没谈的情景。\n借用原文：perhaps it would be sensible to prime yourself with a cup before the exam", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What tip does the speaker give on learning?", "options": ["A) Discover when you can learn best.", "B) Change your time of study daily.", "C) Give yourself a double bonus afterwards.", "D) Follow the example of a marathon runner."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Discover when you can learn best.\n【定位】But this does not apply to everyone, so it is essential to establish your own rhythm.\n但这并不适用于每个人,所以确立你自己的(学习)节律是至关重要的。\n【信号】转折 but + 强调 essential 双信号。本段在说\"多数人上午学下午巩固\"之后用 But this\ndoes not apply to everyone, so it is essential to establish your own rhythm. 一转,by\nit is essential 加重语气点出真正的建议\"建立你自己的节律\";转折后 + essential 强调,正是\n答案句的双重信号。\n【替换】it is essential to establish your own rhythm ↔ Discover when you can learn best\nestablish your own rhythm=找到自己学得最好的时段\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\nB\"每天更换学习时间\"蹭了录音 at different times of the day 的字面,可录音说\"在一天不同\n时段试,看哪个时段记得最牢\"是为了\"找到并固定自己的最佳时段\",而非\"天天换\";B 把\"找固定\n时段\"反说成\"天天变\",与原意相反。\n借用原文：learning a set number of lines of poetry at different times of the day\nC) 原词陷阱\nC\"事后给自己双倍奖励\"搬来录音第4段的 a double bonus,但那个 double bonus 说的\n是\"休息带来的双重好处\",并非\"给自己奖励\";而且它属于第4段\"避免疲劳战、多休息\"的话题,\n不是本题落点,C 既偷换概念又错段。\n借用原文：they offer a double bonus\nD) 与原文相反\nD\"效仿马拉松选手\"蹭了录音第4段的 marathon,可录音说的是 Avoid learning\nmarathons(避免马拉松式的学习),是叫你别那样学;D 取了相反方向,把\"要避免\"听成了\"要效\n仿\"。\n借用原文：Avoid learning marathons—they do not make the best use of your mind", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What does the speaker do?", "options": ["A) He is a politician.", "B) He is a businessman.", "C) He is a sociologist.", "D) He is an economist."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） He is a sociologist.\n【定位】It concerns not only us sociologists, but also economists, politicians and business people.\n（贫困）不仅关乎我们这些社会学家，也关乎经济学家、政治家和商人。\n【信号】us+职业的自我归类。关键信号是 us sociologists 里的 us（我们）——说话人用“我们社\n会学家“把自己算进 sociologists 这一类，这就是自我定位。后面 economists, politicians\nand business people 用 also（也）领出，是“别人”，不是说话人自己。\n【替换】us sociologists ↔ He is a sociologist.\nus+职业=本人即该职业\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nHe is a politician. 盗用了录音里念到的 politicians，但那是用 also 领出的“也关乎的对\n象”，是别人，不是说话人本人，听到 politicians 就选即中陷阱。\n借用原文：economists, politicians and business people\nB) 原词陷阱\nHe is a businessman. 对应录音 business people，同样是 also 后面列举的“也关心此事的\n人”，并非说话人自指。\n借用原文：also economists, politicians and business people\nD) 原词陷阱\nHe is an economist. 是最迷惑的——economists 紧跟在 also 之后第一个被念到（but\nalso economists），与社会学家是研究相近领域的近邻，容易混；但它前面是 also，仍属\n“也关乎的对象”，说话人本人是 us sociologists。\n借用原文：but also economists", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "Where does the speaker say we can find extreme poverty?", "options": ["A) In slums.", "B) In Africa.", "C) In pre-industrial societies.", "D) In developing countries."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） In developing countries.\n【定位】Where does extreme poverty occur? Well, you can find it only in developing countries.\n极端贫困发生在哪里？嗯，你只能在发展中国家找到它。\n【信号】自问自答+occur/find in。录音先自抛设问 Where does extreme poverty occur?，紧接\n着自答 you can find it only in developing countries——设问句后的那句应答就是答案\n句，地点触发词 find it … in 后面紧跟的地名即为落点。\n【替换】in developing countries ↔ In developing countries.\n原词直接复现\n【排除】A) 未提及\nIn slums.（贫民窟）听上去与“贫困“语义相关，很容易凭常识脑补，但录音讲极端贫困地点\n时只说 developing countries，从未出现 slums 一词，属考生用常识替代文本。\nB) 未提及\nIn Africa. 同样未在录音中出现。考生易把“极端贫困“与非洲挂钩，但这是背景常识，不是录\n音内容，全篇没念 Africa。\nC) 未提及\nIn pre-industrial societies.（前工业社会）是个像模像样的学术说法，但录音通篇没说极端\n贫困发生在前工业社会，属凭“听着专业“误选。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What do we learn about American people living in relative poverty?", "options": ["A) They have no access to health care, let alone entertainment or recreation.", "B) Their income is less than 50% of the national average family income.", "C) They work extra hours to have their basic needs met.", "D) Their children cannot afford to go to private schools."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Their income is less than 50% of the national average family income.\n【定位】For example, in the United States, a family can be considered poor if their income is less than\n50% of the national average family income.\n例如，在美国，如果一个家庭的收入低于全国家庭平均收入的50%，就可以被视为贫困。\n【信号】美国举例+50%数字。信号有二：一是 in the United States 正好点中题干限定的“美国”；\n二是数字 50% 出现（less than 50% of the national average family income）——预读\n时已标星的数字落点在此兑现，数字句几乎必是答案句。\n【替换】their income is less than 50% of the national average family income ↔\nTheir income is less than 50% of the national average family income.\n原句几乎逐字搬入选项\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nThey have no access to health care, let alone entertainment or recreation. 重组了本\n段后文 they lack access to cultural guts, entertainment and recreation 与 do not have\naccess to quality health care 的原词，确实在讲相对贫困者，但题目问的是“我们了解到什\n么“中与判定标准对应的那条，A 偷换了状况侧面，且把“缺乏优质医疗“夸大成“完全没有医\n疗”。\n借用原文：they lack access to cultural guts, entertainment and recreation. They also do not have\naccess to quality health care\nC) 张冠李戴\nThey work extra hours to have their basic needs met. 听着像贫困者的常态，但录音讲\n相对贫困者从未提“加班才够基本需求”；basic needs 一词是前文讲极端、中度贫困时反复\n用的（cannot meet basic needs、basic needs are met），被挪来安到美国相对贫困者\n头上，属张冠李戴。\n借用原文：basic needs are met but barely\nD) 未提及\nTheir children cannot afford to go to private schools. 里的 private schools 全篇没出\n现；录音讲极端贫困时说 cannot afford education for their children，考生可能把“供不\n起教育“脑补成“上不起私校”，属无中生有式过度具体化。\n借用原文：They cannot afford education for their children.", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) classified", "B) consciously", "C) dramatically", "D) exotic", "E) identified", "F) inherent", "G) marine", "H) novel", "I) potential", "J) predators", "K) referred", "L) species", "M) specifically", "N) varieties", "O) volunteers"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) referred\n动词\n【词性槽】be ___ to as 固定结构，填动词过去分词\n【依据】refer to A as B = 把 A 称作 B，被动即 be referred to as\n句意是“这种现象常被称为‘首夜效应’”，正是固定搭配 is often __(26)__ to as the \"first-\nnight-effect\" 的填法；refer 与 to as 是死搭。\n【竞争词】A) classified classify 接 as 而非 to as，搭配不成立\nclassify A as B（把 A 归类为 B）中间没有 to，原句明摆着是 to as，硬填 classified 会多出一\n个介词 to，搭配崩盘。\nE) identified identify 也接 as，不接 to as\nbe identified as 表示“被认定为”，同样没有中间的 to；和句中 to as 框架不吻合，结构上直接\n出局。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·be ___ to as 固定结构，填动"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) classified", "B) consciously", "C) dramatically", "D) exotic", "E) identified", "F) inherent", "G) marine", "H) novel", "I) potential", "J) predators", "K) referred", "L) species", "M) specifically", "N) varieties", "O) volunteers"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) volunteers\n名词\n【词性槽】of 后接复数名词作所有者\n【依据】实验受试者 = volunteers，最贴“被研究的人”\n本段讲布朗大学的研究，给右耳播声音观察是否唤醒，符合学术实验中“志愿者/受试者”的常\n用表述，与 Researchers from Brown University found 的实验语境一致。\n【竞争词】J) predators “捕食者”与实验对象身份不符\npredators 是“掠食动物”，本段没有捕食情节，且把声音放进捕食者耳朵也不构成实验逻辑。\nL) species “物种”是抽象类属，不与个体被试对应\nspecies 表“物种”这个分类层级，不会被“给右耳播放声音”这种个体操作直接作用，语境不匹配。\nN) varieties “种类/品种”过于抽象且不指人\nvarieties 多指品种/类型，既不指代实验中的人，也不与具体的“right ears”自然搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·of 后接复数名词作所有者"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) classified", "B) consciously", "C) dramatically", "D) exotic", "E) identified", "F) inherent", "G) marine", "H) novel", "I) potential", "J) predators", "K) referred", "L) species", "M) specifically", "N) varieties", "O) volunteers"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) specifically\n副词\n【词性槽】It was ___ observed，副词修饰动词 observed\n【依据】specifically = 具体地说，引出对前句的具体说明\n上一段刚说 a network in the left hemisphere of the brain \"remained more active\"，\n本句给出更细的实验观察“左脑在深度睡眠时更活跃”，正是“具体而言/特别地观察到”的连接\n关系。\n【竞争词】C) dramatically “戏剧性地观察到”修饰对象错位\ndramatically 描述变化幅度（dramatically increase/change），不修饰 observe 这种中性动\n作；硬填会让“观察”这个动作带上不合理的戏剧色彩。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·It was ___ observed，"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) classified", "B) consciously", "C) dramatically", "D) exotic", "E) identified", "F) inherent", "G) marine", "H) novel", "I) potential", "J) predators", "K) referred", "L) species", "M) specifically", "N) varieties", "O) volunteers"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) novel\n形容词\n【词性槽】a ___ environment，冠词与名词之间填形容词\n【依据】novel = new/unfamiliar，与开篇 a new place 同义改写\n全文开头 Half of your brain stays alert and prepared for danger when you sleep in a\nnew place，本句正是对它的概括复述，novel environment 即“陌生新环境”，是 new\nplace 的同义改写，命中六级最爱考的“同义替换”。\n【竞争词】D) exotic “异国情调的环境”范围太窄、色彩偏旅游\nexotic 偏“异域风情”，本文讲的是任何陌生新环境（甚至同城新酒店），并非异国，与 a new\nplace 不等价。\nF) inherent “固有的环境”语义不通\ninherent 修饰抽象属性（risk/quality），不修饰 environment；inherent environment 不\n成话。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 D) —— novel 与 exotic 都含“非本地/不熟悉”味。in a __(29)__\nenvironment 处考生易选 exotic；防坑：盯开篇 a new place 的同义改写——“新的”用\nnovel，不一定异国情调。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·a ___ environment，冠词"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) classified", "B) consciously", "C) dramatically", "D) exotic", "E) identified", "F) inherent", "G) marine", "H) novel", "I) potential", "J) predators", "K) referred", "L) species", "M) specifically", "N) varieties", "O) volunteers"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) potential\n形容词\n【词性槽】any ___ danger，限定词 + 名词之间是形容词\n【依据】potential danger = 潜在危险，是固定搭配\n“防御任何潜在危险”是英语里的死搭配；与上句 the brain partly remains alert 警觉防御的\n逻辑相符——警觉是为了应对“尚未发生但可能出现”的威胁。\n【竞争词】D) exotic “异国危险”与睡眠警觉语境无关\nexotic danger 不是常规搭配，也与“在新住所半边大脑警觉”这种泛化场景不匹配。\nF) inherent “固有危险”指向“事物本身自带”，与本文“外来威胁”逻辑反\ninherent danger 强调“内在固有”的危险（如行业风险），而本文讲的是**陌生环境里可能出现\n的外部威胁**，方向不符。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·any ___ danger，限定词 +"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) classified", "B) consciously", "C) dramatically", "D) exotic", "E) identified", "F) inherent", "G) marine", "H) novel", "I) potential", "J) predators", "K) referred", "L) species", "M) specifically", "N) varieties", "O) volunteers"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) identified\n动词\n【词性槽】has been ___ in humans，被动完成时缺过去分词\n【依据】identify = 识别/发现/确认，与“首次在人体被发现”最贴\n句意为“这是‘首夜效应’的不同脑状态首次在人体身上被**识别/发现**”，identify 含“辨识出\n（某现象的存在）”，正与 the first time 的“首次发现”呼应；与上文 The researchers\nbelieve this is the first time 形成“首次识别”的语义链。\n【竞争词】A) classified classify = 归类，但本句不在“分类”而在“发现”\nclassified 强调“将……归入某一类别”，需要有明确的类别体系；而原文说的是“首次在人身上**观\n察/确认**这一现象”，并非给它做分类，方向偏。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 A) —— classified 与 identified 都是动词分词、都沾“认定”。考生\n容易在 has been __(31)__ in humans. 选 classified；防坑：问自己“原文是在做分类，还\n是在做发现/识别？”——本句是“首次在人身上发现”，identify 才对位。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·has been ___ in huma"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) classified", "B) consciously", "C) dramatically", "D) exotic", "E) identified", "F) inherent", "G) marine", "H) novel", "I) potential", "J) predators", "K) referred", "L) species", "M) specifically", "N) varieties", "O) volunteers"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) species\n名词\n【词性槽】Some animal ___ also display，名词作主语中心词\n【依据】animal species = 动物物种，是固定搭配\nanimal species 是英语里描述“动物物种”的标准说法，且 species 单复同形，正好对应\ndisplay 这个复数谓语；与下文 For example, dolphins, as well as other __(33)__ animals\n举例呼应——“某些动物物种也表现出该现象，例如海豚”。\n【竞争词】N) varieties varieties 多指“品种/花色”，不指动物分类层级\nanimal varieties 不是科学语境里的标准表述（varieties 常用于产品、花卉品种）；说“动物物\n种”地道的搭配是 species，varieties 显得不专业。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·Some animal ___ also"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) classified", "B) consciously", "C) dramatically", "D) exotic", "E) identified", "F) inherent", "G) marine", "H) novel", "I) potential", "J) predators", "K) referred", "L) species", "M) specifically", "N) varieties", "O) volunteers"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) marine\n形容词\n【词性槽】other ___ animals，形容词修饰名词作前置定语\n【依据】marine animals = 海洋动物，与海豚同类\n海豚 dolphins 属于海洋哺乳动物，“以及其他海洋动物”最自然，与上下文 dolphins, as\nwell as other __(33)__ animals 的并列结构吻合。\n【竞争词】D) exotic “异国情调的动物”与生物学语境违和\nexotic animals 强调“外来/罕见的”，是旅游/宠物语境用语，与本段“海豚等海洋动物的睡眠机\n制”的科学话题不合。\nF) inherent “固有的动物”语义不通\ninherent 修饰抽象属性（如 inherent risk/right），不修饰生物个体；inherent animals 不\n成话。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·other ___ animals，形容"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) classified", "B) consciously", "C) dramatically", "D) exotic", "E) identified", "F) inherent", "G) marine", "H) novel", "I) potential", "J) predators", "K) referred", "L) species", "M) specifically", "N) varieties", "O) volunteers"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) consciously\n副词\n【词性槽】always 与 control 之间，副词修饰动词\n【依据】“有意识地控制呼吸”才解释“不主动就会淹死”\n后句 Without keeping the brain active while sleeping, they would probably drown.\n表明海豚必须**有意识地**控制呼吸，否则会淹死；consciously 与“保持大脑活跃”因果\n相连。\n【竞争词】C) dramatically “戏剧性地控制呼吸”语义荒诞\ndramatically 描述程度变化幅度大，用于 increase/change 这类动词，搭配 control 不通，与\n“否则会淹死”的逻辑链也无关。\nM) specifically “特别地控制呼吸”指向不明\nspecifically 用于“具体地说/特指”，强调精确指代，无法解释为何不睡觉就淹死，语义指向\n跑偏。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·always 与 control 之间，"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) classified", "B) consciously", "C) dramatically", "D) exotic", "E) identified", "F) inherent", "G) marine", "H) novel", "I) potential", "J) predators", "K) referred", "L) species", "M) specifically", "N) varieties", "O) volunteers"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) predators\n名词\n【词性槽】look out for ___，介词后接名词\n【依据】“留心提防捕食者”才与“保持警觉/防御危险”同向\n全文主线就是 the brain partly remains alert so that humans can defend themselves\nagainst any __(30)__ danger，海豚保持警觉的对象正是 predators 捕食者，语义闭环。\n【竞争词】L) species “留心物种”语义空泛，无危险所指\nspecies 是“物种”这种中性分类词，不带威胁色彩，与 look out for（提防）情感方向不匹配。\nN) varieties “留心品种”不构成提防对象\nvarieties 同样中性偏抽象，无法承担“被防”的语义角色。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·look out for ___，介词后"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Middle school is a crucial period when students may become keenly interested in advanced mathematics.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Middle school + advanced mathematics\n【定位】\"Middle school is an important age because students have enough math capability to solve\nadvanced problems, but they haven't really decided what they want to do with their lives,\"\nsaid Loh. \"They often get hooked then.\"\nLoh 说：\"中学是一个很重要的年龄段，因为学生已经具备解决高阶数学问题的能力，但他\n们还没真正决定将来要做什么。\" \"他们往往就在这时候被迷住。\"\n【改写】Middle school is a crucial period ↔ Middle school is an important age\ncrucial = important，period ≈ age\nstudents may become keenly interested in advanced mathematics ↔ They often get hooked then\nget hooked = 被迷住 = keenly interested", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Elite high school math competitions are attracting more interest throughout the United States.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Elite high school math competitions 关注度上升\n【定位】Interest in elite high school math competitions has grown in recent years, and in light of last\nsummer's U.S. win at the International Math Olympiad (IMO)—the first for an American\nteam in more than two decades—the trend is likely to continue.\n近年来，人们对精英高中数学竞赛的兴趣不断上升；考虑到去年夏天美国队在国际数学奥\n林匹克竞赛（IMO）夺冠——这是美国队二十多年来的首胜——这一趋势很可能会延续\n下去。\n【改写】Elite high school math competitions are attracting more interest ↔\nInterest in elite high school math competitions has grown in recent years\n兴趣增长 = 吸引更多兴趣（主被动换装）\nthroughout the United States ↔ last summer's U.S. win at the International Math Olympiad\nU.S. = throughout the United States", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Math circles provide students with access to advanced-math training by university professors.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Math circles + university professors\n【定位】In these groups, which came out of an Eastern European tradition of developing young talent,\nprofessors teach promising K-12 students advanced mathematics for several hours after\nschool or on weekends.\n在这些（数学圈）小组里——它们源自东欧培养年轻人才的传统——教授们在放学后或周末\n给有潜力的 K-12 学生讲授数小时的高阶数学。\n【改写】Math circles provide students with access to advanced-math training ↔\nprofessors teach promising K-12 students advanced mathematics for several hours after school\nor on weekends\nprofessors teach … advanced mathematics = provide access to advanced-math training\nby university professors ↔ professors teach promising K-12 students\nprofessors 来源是大学（上下文已点明）", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Students may take advantage of online resources to learn to solve math problems.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】online resources 学解题\n【定位】Another influential feeder for advanced-math students is an online school called Art of\nProblem Solving, which began about 13 years ago and now has 15,000 users. Students use\nforums to chat, play games, and solve problems together at no cost, or they can pay a few\nhundred dollars to take courses with trained teachers.\n另一条对高阶数学学生很有影响的输送渠道是一所名为 Art of Problem Solving 的网络学\n校，它创立大约 13 年、目前有 1.5 万名用户。学生可以免费在论坛上聊天、玩游戏并共同\n解题，也可以花几百美元选修受过培训的教师所开的课程。\n【改写】online resources ↔ an online school called Art of Problem Solving\n在线学校属于在线资源\nlearn to solve math problems ↔ solve problems together\nsolve problems = 学解数学题\n【辨邻】 D online forums and classes 只是 D 段课外训练形式清单中的一项，并未具体描述\"学\n生借此学解题\"，与 learn to solve math problems 的事件层面对不上；G 段才真正\n展开了\"在论坛上一起解题\"的具体场景。\nStudents attend after-school clubs, summer camps, online forums and classes, and university-based\n\"math circles\", to prepare for the competitions", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "The summer program run by a nonprofit organization has helped many underserved students learn advanced math.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】nonprofit organization 的暑期项目 + underserved students\n【定位】A New York City-based nonprofit called Bridge to Enter Mathematics runs a residential\nsummer program aimed at getting underserved students, mostly black and Hispanic, working\ntoward math and science careers.\n一家总部在纽约市、名为 Bridge to Enter Mathematics 的非营利组织开办了一个住宿式暑\n期项目，目标是帮助以黑人和西裔为主的弱势学生走向数学和科学领域的职业道路。\n【改写】The summer program run by a nonprofit organization ↔\nA New York City-based nonprofit called Bridge to Enter Mathematics runs a residential\nsummer program\nsummer program run by a nonprofit = 非营利组织开办的暑期项目\nhelped many underserved students learn advanced math ↔\nresidential summer program aimed at getting underserved students, mostly black and\nHispanic, working toward math and science careers\nunderserved students 原样复现 + advanced math 由后文具体活动支撑\n【辨邻】 H held by the Mathematical Association of America 这种学会主办与陈述句要\n求的 nonprofit organization 不是一回事；H 段的入选标准是 performance\non written tests and at the USA Math Olympiad，受众是顶尖选手，而非\nunderserved students。\nThe Math Olympiad Summer Training Program is a three-week math camp held by the\nMathematical Association of America that leads straight to the international championship and is\nfree for those who make it.\nM said Daniel Zaharopol, the founder and executive director of the program 表明\nM 段只是 L 段项目的创始人接着发表观点，has helped many underserved\nstudents learn advanced math 这一事实本身仍记在 L 段。\nsaid Daniel Zaharopol, the founder and executive director of the program", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Winners of local contests will participate in the national math competition for free.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】local contests 胜出者免费参加全国赛\n【定位】Students join a team through their schools, which provide a volunteer coach and pay a\nnominal fee to send students to regional and state competitions. The 224 students who make it\nto the national competition get an all-expenses-paid trip.\n学生通过学校加入参赛队，学校提供志愿教练并交一笔象征性费用，把学生送去参加地区和\n州的比赛。最终晋级全国总决赛的 224 名学生获得全程免费的差旅。\n【改写】Winners of local contests ↔ The 224 students who make it to the national competition\n晋级到全国赛者 = 地方赛胜出者\nparticipate in the national math competition for free ↔ get an all-expenses-paid trip\nall-expenses-paid = for free\n【辨邻】 H The Math Olympiad Summer Training Program…and is free for those who make\nit 中 free 适用的是暑期训练营，而非 national math competition；选拔标准\nperformance on written tests and at the USA Math Olympiad 也与陈述句的\"地方\n赛胜出\"对不上。\nOnly about 50 students are invited based on their performance on written tests and at the USA\nMath Olympiad.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Many schools don't place academic competitions at the top of their priority list.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】schools 不把学术竞赛列为优先\n【定位】Many schools simply don't prioritize academic competitions.\n很多学校根本就不把学术竞赛放在优先位置。\n【改写】Many schools don't place academic competitions at the top of their priority list ↔\nMany schools simply don't prioritize academic competitions\ndon't prioritize = don't place at the top of priority list（动名互换）\n【辨邻】 K Teachers…may not know about advanced-math opportunities like Math Counts\n—and those who do may not have support or feel trained to lead them 主语是\nTeachers、判断是\"不知道/没受过训练\"，与陈述句要求的\"Many schools 不把学术竞\n赛列入优先\"在主体和判断维度都不同。\nTeachers in low-income urban and rural areas with no history of participating in math competitions\nmay not know about advanced-math opportunities like Math Counts", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Contestants of elite high school math competitions are mostly Asian and white students from well-off families.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】选手主体为亚裔白人富裕家庭\n【定位】But will such contests, which are overwhelmingly dominated by Asian and white students\nfrom middle-class and affluent families, become any more diverse?\n但是，这些被亚裔和白人、来自中产与富裕家庭的学生压倒性主导的竞赛，会不会变得更加\n多元？\n【改写】Contestants of elite high school math competitions are mostly Asian and white students ↔\nsuch contests, which are overwhelmingly dominated by Asian and white students\noverwhelmingly dominated by = mostly are\nfrom well-off families ↔ from middle-class and affluent families\nmiddle-class and affluent → well-off（具体并列概括为上位词）", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Some math training programs primarily focus on raising students' math scores.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】math training programs 主要为了提分\n【定位】\"If you look at a lot of low-income communities in the United States, there are programs that\nare serving them, but they're primarily centered around 'Let's get these kids' grades up', and\nnot around 'Let's get these kids access to the same kinds of opportunities as more-affluent\nkids,'\" said Daniel Zaharopol, the founder and executive director of the program.\n项目创始人兼执行主任 Daniel Zaharopol 说：\"在美国很多低收入社区，确实有一些服务于\n这些孩子的项目，但它们的重心主要是把这些孩子的成绩提上来，而不是让他们享有和富裕\n家庭孩子同样的机会。\"\n【改写】Some math training programs ↔ there are programs that are serving them\n服务低收入社区的程序 = some math training programs\nprimarily focus on raising students' math scores ↔\nthey're primarily centered around 'Let's get these kids' grades up'\nprimarily centered around = primarily focus on；get grades up = raise math scores\n【辨邻】 L L 段写的是 Bridge to Enter Mathematics 项目的自身做法，而陈述句 Some math\ntraining programs primarily focus on raising students' math scores 是对\"其他项\n目\"现状的批评式立场判断，源头在 M 段。\nAbout 250 students so far have gone through the program, which receives funding from the Jack\nKent Cooke Foundation.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Some intensive summer programs are very expensive but most of them provide scholarships.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】intensive summer programs 贵但多有奖学金\n【定位】Some are pricey—a three-week intensive program can cost $4,500 or more—but most\noffer scholarships.\n有些营很贵——一个为期三周的强化营可能要花 4500 美元甚至更多——但大多数都提供奖\n学金。\n【改写】Some intensive summer programs are very expensive ↔\nSome are pricey—a three-week intensive program can cost $4,500 or more\npricey = very expensive\nbut most of them provide scholarships ↔ but most offer scholarships\noffer = provide，结构对结构", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What contribution did the women designers make to American fashion?", "options": ["A) They made some improvements on the traditional Parisian design.", "B) They formulated a dress code with distinctive American features.", "C) They came up with a brand new set of design procedures.", "D) They made originality a top priority in their fashion design."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What contribution did the women designers make to American fashion? 问的是已发\n生的客观事实贡献，不含 infer/suggest/imply 一类推断信号，属典型细节题。\n【拆句】Q46 是疑问句 What contribution did A make to B 的标准句式，主干 women designers\nmade contribution to American fashion，结构平铺直叙、无陷阱。\n【定位】These designers established the modern dress code, letting playsuits and other active wear\noutfits suffice for casual clothing, allowing pants to enter the wardrobe, and prizing\nrationalism and versatility in dress, in contradiction to dressing for an occasion or allotment\nof the day.\n这些设计师建立了现代着装规则，让连体运动装等活动便服足以充当休闲服，让长裤走进衣\n橱，崇尚穿着的理性与多用途，反对按场合或一天的时段穿衣。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nmade some improvements on the traditional Parisian design 称是在巴黎设计基础\n上改良，但 liberated American fashion from the confines of Parisian design 明确\n说是「挣脱」巴黎设计，不是改良延续。\nB) ✓ 正确\nThese designers established the modern dress code ↔ They formulated a dress code\nliberated American fashion from the confines of Parisian design ↔\nwith distinctive American features\n预判「建立规则」与 formulated a dress code 完全对位，且「区别于巴黎」对应\ndistinctive American features，是定位句的双重同义替换。\nC) 无中生有\ncame up with a brand new set of design procedures 指「全新的设计流程」，原文\n谈的是着装规则与穿着理念，从未涉及「设计流程」这一对象。\nD) 偷换概念\nmade originality a top priority in their fashion design 把贡献偷换为「把原创性当首\n要追求」，原文强调的是 prizing rationalism and versatility——理性与多用途，而非\n「原创」。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What do we learn about American designer sportswear?", "options": ["A) It imitated the European model.", "B) It laid emphasis on women's beauty.", "C) It represented genuine American art.", "D) It was a completely new invention."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about American designer sportswear? 问对象的客观属性，不含\ninfer/imply，是「请读出原文怎么说」的细节题。\n【拆句】Q47 主干 we learn about American designer sportswear，about 引出话题对象，结构\n简单、无陷阱。\n【定位】Designer sportswear was not modeled on that of Europe, as \"modern art\" would later be; it\nwas genuinely invented and developed in America.\n设计师运动装并不像后来「现代艺术」那样以欧洲为模板，它是地地道道在美国发明并发展\n起来的。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nimitated the European model 称「模仿欧洲模式」，但原文 was not modeled on\nthat of Europe 明确否定这一点。\nB) 偷换概念\nlaid emphasis on women's beauty 把重点偷换为「女性之美」，但本段强调的是\nproblem-solving ingenuity and realistic lifestyle applications——实用而非美。\nC) 偷换概念\nrepresented genuine American art 把「在美国地道发明的运动装」偷换为「真正的美\n国艺术」，原文 as \"modern art\" would later be 反而是把 sportswear 与 modern\nart 区分开来，并未把 sportswear 称作艺术。\nD) ✓ 正确\nit was genuinely invented and developed in America ↔ It was a completely new invention\n预判「美国地道发明」与 a completely new invention 的名词化表述完全等值，是定位\n句的标准同义改写。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What characterized American designer sportswear?", "options": ["A) Pursuit of beauty.", "B) Decorative closings.", "C) Ease of care.", "D) Fabric quality."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What characterized American designer sportswear? 是问哪条属性是它的特征，是属性\n匹配类细节题。\n【拆句】Q48 主干 What characterized X，疑问代词 what 作主语，结构简单。\n【定位】Ease of care was most important: summer dresses and outfits, in particular, were chiefly\ncotton, readily capable of being washed and pressed at home.\n易于打理最为重要：夏季连衣裙和服装尤其以棉质为主，在家就能轻松洗烫。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nPursuit of beauty 不是本段为运动装定义的特征；本段的对照面恰恰是「设计师以实\n用、易打理来打破传统时装对美的单一追求」。\nB) 与原文相反\nDecorative closings 称「装饰性的开合设计」，但原文 Closings were simple,\npractical, and accessible 明确说开合是简洁实用、易操作，与「装饰性」相反。\nC) ✓ 正确\nEase of care was most important ↔ Ease of care\n预判 Ease of care 与定位句 Ease of care was most important 一字不差，是最直接的\n特征匹配。\nD) 以偏概全\nFabric quality 称「面料品质」，原文虽提到 chiefly cotton，但这只是 ease of care 的\n下属支撑细节，是为「易打理」服务的，而非被标举为整体特征。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What occurred in the design of women's apparel in America during the 1930s-40s?", "options": ["A) A shift of emphasis from beauty to utility.", "B) The emulation of traditional Parisian design.", "C) A search for balance between tradition and novelty.", "D) The involvement of more women in fashion design."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What occurred in the design of women's apparel in America during the 1930s-40s?\n问的是该时段发生了什么客观事件，是细节题。\n【拆句】Q49 疑问句 what 作主语，介词短语 in the design of women's apparel + in America +\nduring the 1930s-40s 三层状语并列限定，结构略繁但语义清晰。\n【定位】Of course, much of this argument in the 1930s-40s was advanced because there was little or\nno experience in justifying apparel(服装) on the basis of utility. If Paris was cast aside, the\ntradition of beauty was also to some degree slighted.\n当然，1930-40 年代这一论争之所以被推到台面，是因为当时几乎没有以「实用」为衣服辩\n护的先例。一旦撇开巴黎，「美」的传统也在一定程度上被冷落了。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nmuch of this argument in the 1930s-40s was advanced because there was little or no experience\nin justifying apparel(服装) on the basis of utility\n↔ A shift of emphasis from beauty to utility\nIf Paris was cast aside, the tradition of beauty was also to some degree slighted ↔ from beauty\n预判「美让位实用」与 A shift of emphasis from beauty to utility 完全对位；A 选项\n的 from/to 双向结构精准地承接了原文「美冷落 + 实用兴起」的双向对照。\nB) 与原文相反\nThe emulation of traditional Parisian design 称「效仿传统巴黎设计」，但原文 If\nParis was cast aside 与首段 liberated American fashion from the confines of\nParisian design 都明确否认这一方向。\nC) 无中生有\nA search for balance between tradition and novelty 称「在传统与新异之间寻求平\n衡」，但原文给出的是「明显的方向性替代」（美让位实用），并无任何「寻求平衡」\n的表述。\nD) 偷换概念\nThe involvement of more women in fashion design 把发生的事偷换为「更多女性参\n与设计」，但原文并未把 1930-40 年代描述为女性参与人数增加的时段；女设计师群体\n在首段就已作为既定背景登场，本段聚焦的是「美 vs 实用」的论辩。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What do we learn about designers of American sportswear?", "options": ["A) They catered to the taste of the younger generation.", "B) They radically changed people's concept of beauty.", "C) They advocated equity between men and women.", "D) They became rivals of their Parisian counterparts."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about designers of American sportswear? 问的是对设计师群体的客\n观陈述，不含 infer/imply，仍走细节题路径但定位区间偏向末段总评。\n【拆句】Q50 主干 we learn about designers of American sportswear，about 引出话题对象，\n与第 47 题同结构、无陷阱。\n【定位】They were the pioneers of gender equity, in their useful, adaptable clothing, which was both\nmade for the masses and capable of self-expression.\n他们是性别平等的先驱——他们那些实用、可适应的衣服既面向大众，又能用于自我表达。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\ncatered to the taste of the younger generation 称「迎合年轻一代品味」，原文从未\n提及「年轻人」或「世代差异」这一维度。\nB) 过度推断\nradically changed people's concept of beauty 把「证明时装是实用设计艺术」推到\n「彻底改变人们的美学观念」上去，跨度过大。原文 fashion is a genuine design art,\nanswering to the demanding needs of service 强调的是「时装的设计艺术属性以服\n务为本」，不等于「彻底改变了大众的美学观念」。\nC) ✓ 正确\nThey were the pioneers of gender equity ↔ They advocated equity between men and women\n预判「倡导性别平等」与 advocated equity between men and women 完全对位，是\n末段升华句的标准同义改写。\nD) 无中生有\nbecame rivals of their Parisian counterparts 称「成为巴黎同行的竞争对手」，原文\n一直说的是「美国时装从巴黎设计中解放出来」「设计师运动装不师从欧洲」，强调的\n是独立而非对抗式竞争，从未出现 rivalry 的表述。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What is the impact of rubbish dumps on wildlife?", "options": ["A) They have forced white storks to search for safer winter shelters.", "B) They have seriously polluted the places where birds spend winter.", "C) They have accelerated the reproduction of some harmful insects.", "D) They have changed the previous migration habits of certain birds."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What is the impact of rubbish dumps on wildlife?问的是一个具体客观事实——X对Y产\n生了什么影响，不带infer／suggest／imply类信号词。\n【拆句】主干是What is the impact of rubbish dumps on wildlife?——What作表语提问，主语是\nthe impact，of rubbish dumps是impact的修饰（影响的发出者），on wildlife是impact\n的指向对象（影响的承受者）。句子平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Massive rubbish dumps and sprawling landfills constitute one of the more uncomfortable\nimpacts that humans have on wildlife. They have led some birds to give up on migration.\nInstead of flying thousands of miles in search of food, they make the waste sites their winter\nfeeding grounds.\n巨大的垃圾堆和肆意扩张的填埋场，是人类对野生动物造成的较为令人不安的影响之一。它\n们已经导致一些鸟类放弃迁徙。这些鸟不再飞越数千英里去寻找食物，而是把垃圾场当成它\n们的越冬觅食地。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nThey have forced white storks to search for safer winter shelters.说鸟去寻找「更安\n全的」避寒处，但原文明说鸟把垃圾场当作winter feeding grounds，且垃圾场被Flack\n描述为very risky，恰恰不安全，与原文相反。\nB) 无中生有\nThey have seriously polluted the places where birds spend winter.说垃圾场严重污\n染了鸟的越冬地，但原文谈的是「垃圾场本身成了越冬地」，并未说垃圾场污染了「鸟\n原本的越冬地」，无对应。\nC) 张冠李戴\nThey have accelerated the reproduction of some harmful insects.说垃圾场加速了\n害虫繁殖，但White storks feed on locusts and other insects that can become\npests的主语是white storks以蝗虫等为食，谈的是鸟为生态服务的作用；「加速繁殖」\n既未提，主语也错位。\nD) ✓ 正确\nled some birds to give up on migration ↔\nchanged the previous migration habits of certain birds\n预判要的是「让鸟不再迁徙」，changed the previous migration habits of certain\nbirds正是这层意思的上位表述（「放弃」是「改变」的子集），且无其他选项触及「迁\n徙习惯」。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What do we learn about birds following the traditional migration routes?", "options": ["A) They can multiply at an accelerating rate.", "B) They can better pull through the winter.", "C) They help humans kill harmful insects.", "D) They are more likely to be at risk of dying."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about birds following the traditional migration routes?问的是关于\n某类鸟的客观陈述，是细节复现题。\n【拆句】主干What do we learn about birds，following the traditional migration routes是现在\n分词短语作birds的后置定语，限定「哪类鸟」——是「走传统迁徙路线的」那一类，与\n「停在垃圾场过冬」的那一类对比。\n【定位】Andrea Flack of the Max Planck Institute found that birds following traditional migration\nroutes were more likely to die than German storks that flew only as far as northern Morocco,\nand spent the winter there on rubbish dumps.\nMax Planck研究所的Andrea Flack发现，遵循传统迁徙路线的鸟比那些只飞到摩洛哥北部\n并在那里垃圾场过冬的德国白鹳更容易死亡。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nThey can multiply at an accelerating rate.说传统路线鸟「加速繁殖」，但原文从未涉\n及任何鸟的繁殖率，全段在谈「存活率」。\nB) 与原文相反\nThey can better pull through the winter.说传统路线鸟「更能熬过冬天」，但原文明\n说他们were more likely to die——死亡率更高即更难熬过冬天，方向反了。\nC) 张冠李戴\nThey help humans kill harmful insects.说「帮人类杀害虫」，原文White storks feed\non locusts and other insects that can become pests谈的确是白鹳吃害虫的生态作\n用，但这一作用属于全体白鹳的物种属性，并非特指「走传统路线的鸟」，与Q52题干\n主语错配。\nD) ✓ 正确\nmore likely to die ↔ more likely to be at risk of dying\n预判要的是「更容易死」，D项把die包装成be at risk of dying，比较方向（more\nlikely）与对象（traditional路线的鸟）都对得上，是唯一保留比较方向且语义一致的\n选项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What does Andrea Flack say about the birds overwintering on rubbish dumps?", "options": ["A) They may end up staying there permanently.", "B) They may eat something harmful.", "C) They may evolve new feeding habits.", "D) They may have trouble getting adequate food."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does Andrea Flack say about the birds overwintering on rubbish dumps?问某\n人对某事的具体说法，是引语细节题。\n【拆句】主干What does Andrea Flack say，about the birds是say的宾语介短，overwintering\non rubbish dumps是现在分词后置定语修饰birds——指「在垃圾场过冬的那群鸟」，与传\n统路线鸟相区分。\n【定位】\"It's very risky. The birds can easily eat pieces of plastic or rubber bands and they can die,\"\nsaid Flack. \"And we don't know about the long-term consequences. They might eat something\ntoxic and damage their health. We cannot estimate that yet.\"\n「风险很大。鸟很容易吃下塑料碎片或橡皮筋，然后死掉。」Flack说。「而且我们不知道\n长期后果。它们可能吃下有毒物质而损害健康。我们目前还无法估算这一点。」\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nThey may end up staying there permanently.说鸟可能「永久留在垃圾场」，但原文\n只谈「在垃圾场过冬」（over wintering），是季节性行为，未提任何「永久」信号。\nB) ✓ 正确\nThey might eat something toxic and damage their health. ↔\nThey may eat something harmful.\n预判要的是「可能吃到有害物」，They may eat something harmful.逐词对应原文\nmight eat something toxic，是教科书式的「情态等值+毒→害上位化」组合改写。\nC) 无中生有\nThey may evolve new feeding habits.说鸟「可能进化出新的觅食习惯」，evolve／\nnew feeding habits在原文找不到对应；Flack的话题是健康风险，不是觅食习惯演化。\nD) 与原文相反\nThey may have trouble getting adequate food.说鸟「可能难以获得足够食物」，但\n原文For the birds it's a very convenient way to get food. There are huge clusters\nof organic waste they can feed on明说食物又多又方便，与「难以获得」完全相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What can be inferred about the Spanish birds tagged in the study?", "options": ["A) They gradually lose the habit of migrating in winter.", "B) They prefer rubbish dumps far away to those at home.", "C) They are not attracted to the rubbish dumps on their migration routes.", "D) They join the storks from Germany on rubbish dumps in Morocco."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （推断题）\n【判型】What can be inferred about the Spanish birds tagged in the study?含be inferred信号\n词，明确要求做一步推理而非直接复述。\n【拆句】主干What can be inferred about the Spanish birds，tagged in the study是过去分词作\nSpanish birds的后置定语（被研究标记的那群西班牙鸟），限定研究范围内的样本，不是\n泛指所有西班牙鸟。\n【定位】Landfill sites on the Iberian peninsula have long attracted local white storks, but all of the\nSpanish birds tagged in the study flew across the Sahara desert to the western Sahel.\n伊比利亚半岛上的垃圾填埋场长期以来一直吸引着当地的白鹳，但研究中被标记的所有西班\n牙鸟都飞越撒哈拉沙漠到了西非Sahel。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nThey gradually lose the habit of migrating in winter.说西班牙鸟「逐渐丧失迁徙习\n惯」，但原文恰恰相反——这批鸟「飞越撒哈拉」完成了远距离迁徙，证明迁徙习惯保\n留得好好的，是过推+反向。\nB) 过度推断\nThey prefer rubbish dumps far away to those at home.说鸟「偏好远处的垃圾场胜\n过本地的」，但原文未说这批鸟去Sahel是为了「那里的垃圾场」，他们去的是Sahel\n（越冬地）而非Sahel的垃圾场；同时prefer...to...是主动偏好，原文无主观偏好证据。\nC) ✓ 正确\nall of the Spanish birds tagged in the study flew across the Sahara desert to the western Sahel ↔\nThey are not attracted to the rubbish dumps on their migration routes.\n预判要的是「宁可远飞也不停本地垃圾场」，not attracted to the rubbish dumps on\ntheir migration routes正是把「飞越而过」明示为「未被吸引」，是这一步推断的精准\n表达。\nD) 与原文相反\nThey join the storks from Germany on rubbish dumps in Morocco.说西班牙鸟「在\n摩洛哥垃圾场与德国鸟会合」，但原文明说西班牙鸟flew across the Sahara desert to\nthe western Sahel——他们飞过了撒哈拉到Sahel，根本没停在摩洛哥；摩洛哥垃圾场\n停的是德国鸟。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What is scientists' other concern about white storks feeding on landfills?", "options": ["A) The potential harm to the ecosystem.", "B) The genetic change in the stork species.", "C) The spread of epidemics to their homeland.", "D) The damaging effect on bio-diversity."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What is scientists' other concern问的是科学家的「另一个」具体担忧，是要找原文明示\n的客观陈述。\n【拆句】主干What is scientists' other concern about white storks feeding on landfills?——\nother修饰concern暗示「除前文已提之外」，about white storks feeding on landfills是\nconcern的内容介短，feeding on landfills是现在分词作后置定语修饰storks。\n【定位】But that's not the only uncertainty. Migrating birds affect ecosystems both at home and\nat their winter destinations, and disrupting the traditional routes could have unexpected\nside effects.\n但这不是唯一的不确定性。迁徙鸟同时影响起点和越冬地的生态系统，而扰乱传统迁徙路线\n可能带来意想不到的副作用。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nMigrating birds affect ecosystems both at home and at their winter destinations, and disrupting\nthe traditional routes could have unexpected side effects.\n↔ The potential harm to the ecosystem.\n预判要的是「对生态系统的潜在影响」，A项把affect ecosystems（动词短语）+could\nhave side effects（情态+负面后果）合并为harm to the ecosystem+potential（名词\n+形容词），是双重包装但完全等值。\nB) 无中生有\nThe genetic change in the stork species.说「鹳物种的基因变化」，但原文从未涉及\ngenetic／基因层面的描述，只谈行为（迁徙改变）与生态影响。\nC) 无中生有\nThe spread of epidemics to their homeland.说「流行病传到家乡」，epidemics／流\n行病在全文找不到对应；原文谈的是吃塑料／有毒物的个体健康风险，不是种群间的传\n染病。\nD) 偷换概念\nThe damaging effect on bio-diversity.说「对生物多样性的破坏」，bio-diversity与\necosystems看似近义但概念不同——前者特指物种数量／丰富度，后者指系统整体。原\n文用的是ecosystems（系统），并未谈到bio-diversity（多样性）。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
